5\"","\"-154.506154360490633215227784729994785\"","\"-154.652027611914206548561118063328118\"","\"-157.461341181404736093166639698191084\"","\"-158.549912456449043514908501083113192\"","\"-131.801725258036477304862838982656571\"","\"-105.053538059623911094817176882199950\"","\"-142.872106256483001505776080991788991\"","\"-147.748440661213881505776080991788991\"","\"-147.894313912637454839109414325122324\"","\"-150.703035548991159168603585758151134\"","\"-151.794448103092227622879928111877192\"","\"-125.046260904679661412834266011420572\"","\"-98.2980737062670952027886039109639505\"","\"-136.116641903126185613747508020552992\"","\"-140.992976307857065613747508020552992\"","\"-141.138849559280638947080841353886325\"","\"-143.946978571908858432046032676512172\"","\"-145.041235719892254140061479560172453\"","\"-118.293048521479687930015817459715832\"","\"-91.544861323067121719970155359259211\"","\"-129.363429519926212130929059468848252\"","\"-134.239763924657092130929059468848252\"","\"-134.385637176080665464262392802181585\"","\"-137.193172873589053705713320204275666\"","\"-138.290277934147639382597233906488426\"","\"-111.542090735735073172551571806031805\"","\"1298.60923223152995306045354833330286\"","\"1354.25012065649375250668857034847603\"","\"1421.01918676645031184217059676668383\"","\"1113.09362762702567405181732714283102\"","\"1145.90394824780240596719802106409511\"","\"1184.18265563870859320180883063890319\"","\"SHA256 of normalized effective assumptions and funding; every attempted candidate maps to a retained complete path, including all failures.\"","\"# Rappid Valves (India) Limited\\n## 1. Executive verdict\\n**Hold — unresolved. Research/watchlist only, not eligible for investment consideration on present evidence.** The issuer is Rappid Valves (India) Limited, NSE symbol RAPPID, ISIN INE0MVO01012, not a similarly named valve business. At ₹361 on 19 October 2026, 5,191,984 shares imply equity value ₹187.4306224 crore. This is an SME security with an indivisible 600-share lot.\\n\\nThe commercial story is credible enough to investigate: nominal sales expanded substantially, exports increased, and a September 2026 firm backlog of ₹54.36 crore supports marine demand. But profitable expansion has consumed, rather than generated, cash. FY26 PAT was ₹6.478 crore against CFO of negative ₹10.826 crore. Inventory and receivables absorbed far more capital than the year's incremental sales. A high reported growth history is not sufficient evidence of a self-funding compounder.\\n\\nThe most important unresolved question is not a terminal P/E. It is how existing deposits, customer collections, overdrafts, taxes and supplier payments bridge the audited March balance sheet into the October entry date, then fund deliveries without an assumed rescue. The primary gross-payment model breaches its cash floor before entry. This is an analytical failure to establish funding, **not an assertion that the company actually defaulted in April**. Ordinary return and price-expectation factors therefore remain NS. Even optimistic, explicitly conditional deposit-access and bounded-equity alternatives do not support the required return in the sampled reverse grids.\\n\\n## 2. Research header, gates and data confidence\\nResearch cutoff: **20 October 2026**. Market cutoff: **19 October 2026**. Sources retrieved on 23 October are used only for documents available by the cutoff. No later financial performance is admitted. Ranking eligibility is false while the data and funding gates remain unresolved. Financial basis: standalone Indian accounting reports; FY26 is the latest complete audited year available in this record. July's ₹14.87 crore update is unaudited **sales**, not a filed quarterly revenue-from-operations result. No H1FY27 financial statement is silently synthesized from it.\\n\\n| Gate | Status | Reason |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| G1 | Conditional | Reporting/stewardship weaknesses are evidenced, but not abuse sufficient to establish Fail. |\\n| G2 | Hold — unresolved | Material note, vintage and current cash-availability questions remain unresolved. |\\n| G3 | Hold — unresolved | Scenario failure does not prove actual insolvency; the current cash/deposit/bank bridge is not verified. |\\n| G4 | Conditional | Trade and lot data are verified; lot indivisibility, spread and current restriction gaps limit implementation assurance. |\\n\\nConfidence is high in the exchange price/share/lot/tape extract and latest audited statement arithmetic, medium in economic interpretation, and low in current deposit availability, bank renewal, collection timing and forward returns. Missing facts are ND (not disclosed/verified); an unscoreable factor is NS; a denominator or historical comparison that is not meaningful is NM. These labels are not zeros. Main audit opinions, CARO, IFC, statutory exceptions and management explanations are evaluated separately.\\n\\nThe four gates are not four automatic numerical deductions. No evidenced G1 Fail or G3 Fail has been established, so the corresponding score-of-3 caps are not invoked. The unresolved G2/G3 status nevertheless prevents an investment-ready classification. Reported zero encumbrance in September is a dated positive, not a substitute for bank-liquidity evidence. Sources: [FY26 report](https://www.rappidvalves.in/assets/img/uploads/annual_report/3c0d8-annual-report-2025-26.pdf), [September shareholding filing](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/xbrl/SHP_1736091_12102026034610_WEB.xml).\\n\\n## 3. Scorecard and calculation audit\\n| Composite | Raw | Final display | Coverage | Raw bounds | Effective bounds after cap |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| BQS | 4.50 | 4.5 | 100.00% | 4.500–4.500 | 4.500–4.500 |\\n| GQS | 5.85 | 5.0 | 100.00% | 5.850–5.850 | 5.000–5.000 |\\n| IAS | NS | NS | 55.00% | 2.375–6.425 | 2.375–6.425 |\\n\\n| Factor | Score | Confidence | Evidence and contrary fact |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| GOV | 4 | Medium | Main opinions are unmodified, but India backup exceptions recur in FY24–26 and material filing/note errors persist. IPO acquisition/capex money was redirected to working capital; old statutory dues and the Vinpra relationship warrant scrutiny. Contrary: No auditor-identified fraud or bank default; object variation received shareholder approval. September 2026 filing reports no pledge, NDU or other encumbrance, a dated positive not a blanket assurance. |\\n| MOAT | 5 | Medium | Customer qualification, marine engineering and product-specific factory approvals create useful switching friction. Competitors have comparable industrial products and certifications; metal-price inflation was not fully passed through. Contrary: FY24 top-five customers represented 71.88% of sales. Purchase orders do not establish permanent contracts or market share. |\\n| REV | 8 | Medium | FY21–26 nominal constant-entity operating sales grew from 7.6830956 to 53.233 crore, with no completed acquisition identified. Manufacturing gross value added rose from 9.88681 to 14.06742 to 15.492 crore in FY24–26. Contrary: The pandemic depressed FY21. FY26 growth was 2.13%, with opposing half-year trends. This is nominal constant-scope growth, not verified volume growth: starting score 9, medium-confidence ceiling 8, no trajectory adjustment. |\\n| DUR | 6 | Medium | September firm backlog was 54.36 crore; BHEL orders span five ships through FY28. Machining/testing purchases and US product qualification can broaden access. Contrary: Annual backlog rollforwards, order margins, advances and cancellations are not disclosed. US final approval remains pending. |\\n| CAP | 4 | Medium-low | Operating capital increased much faster than recent NOPAT; the one-year incremental return proxy fell to about 2.10%. The calculation includes machinery and working capital, plus a separately disclosed lagged FY24–25 deployment proxy. Contrary: Project-level commissioning cohorts are not disclosed. The lagged proxy is stronger than the latest increment; score 4 reflects mixed observed returns, not proven destruction of every project. |\\n| CASH | 1 | High | FY24–26 cumulative CFO was negative 26.12514 crore against PAT of 16.64127 crore. FY26 inventory rose 10.50486 crore and receivables 5.59962 crore despite only 1.1084 crore of sales growth. Contrary: Profits and orders are positive, and future collections could recover; neither reverses the observed cash conversion. |\\n| BAL | 3 | Medium | Debt was 17.843 crore against 0.387 crore cash/current-account proxy; deposit availability is unresolved. Gross-payment timing and principal runoff expose funding gaps; the modeled equity rescue is optional and uncommitted. Contrary: Operating interest cover and book equity provide some protection. No actual current default has been established. |\\n| EXEC | 4 | Medium | The February 2025 volume-growth target and June 2025 capex timetable were not demonstrated by FY26 results. The June 2026 equipment timetable remained procurement/advance language in July; the IPO acquisition was not executed. Contrary: Export sales and formal marine orders grew. Management acknowledged the metal-price shock and missed growth. |\\n| EARN | 5 | Medium-low | PAT grew from 6.03686 to 6.478 crore; year-end-share EPS rose from about 11.63 to 12.48 without FY26 issuance. A useful-life estimate change, the FY25 weighted-share presentation and comparative errors limit earnings precision. Contrary: Operating profit supplies most earnings. Removing other income and adding a separate D&A stress still leaves positive profits; cash conversion is not double-penalized here. |\\n| R | NS | Low | The primary base gross-cash path breaches before entry, so an ordinary investment IRR is not valid. Optional post-entry equity cannot repair a past breach or become committed finance. Contrary: Some conditional deposit-access/slow-growth paths can be valued, but they do not establish a supported current base return. |\\n| D | 2 | Medium | Linked margin, collection, inventory and debt-maturity stress overwhelms cash and the bounded rescue. Receivables and inventory are not a liquid equity floor against overdraft funding. Contrary: Product assets, profits and firm orders may retain value. There is no finding of actual insolvency or default. |\\n| P | NS | Low | Every candidate in the bounded cash-linked reverse search is retained, including failed paths. No target is bracketed between adjacent cash-feasible samples in the stated 0–60% growth grids. Contrary: This is not proof that every nonmonotonic root is absent. Improved economics still rely on unverified existing-deposit availability; P remains NS. |\\n| L | 4 | Medium | The lower 60-session mean/median is median turnover 1,955,640 rupees; the five-lakh reference requires 5.1134 sessions at 5% participation, starting band 5. A 600-share lot costs 216,600 rupees, more than twice the 97,782-rupee median daily participation allowance: one-point implementation deduction. Contrary: 59 of 60 sessions traded. Spread and current surveillance clearance are unresolved; 2% and 10% participation sensitivities are provided. |\\n\\nExact Decimal calculations are retained, not reconstructed from displayed rounded scores. BQS = .20×GOV + .20×MOAT + .12×REV + .10×DUR + .12×CAP + .10×CASH + .08×BAL + .08×EXEC = **4.50**. GQS = .40×REV + .20×DUR + .15×EARN + .15×CAP + .10×CASH = **5.85**, capped at **5.00** because persistently weak cash conversion has CASH ≤3. The cap is not an additional factor penalty.\\n\\nIAS = .25×BQS_final + .35×R + .20×D + .10×P + .10×L. Its known weighted sum is **1.925**, known coverage **55%**, and missing weight **45%**. With each missing factor bounded 1–10, IAS is **NS**, bounded **2.375–6.425**. It is not 1.925, not a renormalized known-factor average, and not a midpoint estimate. The displayed composites use one decimal; all formulas use unrounded values.\\n\\n| Hypothetical judgment | BQS raw → final | GQS raw → final | IAS effective bounds (still NS) |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| GOV −1 | 4.30 → 3.00 | 5.85 → 5.00 | 2.000–3.000 |\\n| GOV +1 | 4.70 → 4.70 | 5.85 → 5.00 | 2.425–6.475 |\\n| CAP −1 | 4.38 → 4.38 | 5.70 → 5.00 | 2.345–6.395 |\\n| CAP +1 | 4.62 → 4.62 | 6.00 → 5.00 | 2.405–6.455 |\\n\\nThese are judgment sensitivities, not changes to the actual findings. Hypothetical GOV = 3 triggers the BQS/IAS ceiling of 3, while IAS remains NS and only its effective bounds are capped. Actual GOV remains 4; CAP changes also propagate through nested BQS into IAS. Scores judge different dimensions: CASH records observed conversion; BAL records funding structure; R requires a feasible prospective return; EARN does not repeat the CFO deduction.\\n\\n## 4. Business overview\\nRappid manufactures engineered industrial and marine valves, including ball, gate, globe, butterfly, check and specialized marine applications. Its economic role is machining, assembly, testing, qualification and delivery of specified flow-control equipment, with bought-in materials and components. It is not a royalty or asset-light software model. Different diameters, metallurgy, pressure ratings and customer tests make rupee capacity dependent on product mix; management's approximately ₹120 crore capacity statement is not a standardized physical-unit denominator.\\n\\nCustomer qualification and past delivery matter, particularly where naval/shipyard specifications and approval cycles are involved. However, order execution also requires materials, work in progress, finished goods awaiting approval/dispatch and lengthy receivables. The audited record describes one valve-manufacturing segment, not separately disclosed economics for marine, industrial, export or US opportunities. Segment-level margins and incremental capital are ND. No completed acquisition was identified in the measured growth period; proposed acquisition capital was later redirected.\\n\\nThe prospectus records top-five customer concentration of 71.88% in FY24. Export growth provides diversification, but a new export geography does not by itself remove customer or project concentration. Customer advances and payable credit have not offset the observed cash investment. The company's size and customization can enable responsiveness, but also expose it to single-order timing and metal-price shocks. Sources: SIPO, S26, SCALL, SCL26.\\n\\n## 5. Moat and industry position\\nThe moat is moderate, not proven dominant. Qualification, engineering familiarity and reliable testing create friction against switching an approved valve mid-project. They do not demonstrate permanent pricing power, exclusivity or a defensible market share. Management described roughly ₹10–12 crore of goods withheld from dispatch while private-customer prices were renegotiated following metal inflation; PSU contracts offered less flexibility. That is evidence against treating gross margins as contractually protected.\\n\\nThree economic comparators bound the interpretation, without importing their valuation multiples:\\n- **Kirloskar Brothers** is a much broader, larger pump-and-valve franchise with diverse products, channels and customer sectors. Its scale and service infrastructure show what a stronger commercial moat can look like, but make its consolidated/standalone earnings multiple a poor direct anchor for Rappid. The FY26 virtual report is a qualitative operating benchmark, not a like-for-like accounting peer.\\n- **Hawa Engineers** is a relevant industrial-valve competitor with broad product specifications. Its FY25 report records revenue ₹121.0618 crore and PAT ₹1.5958 crore. That lower-margin historical comparator shows that a valve label does not guarantee high returns. This is FY25, not a fabricated current FY26 comparison.\\n- **Atam Valves** is nearer scale. Its May 2026 audited FY26 results record revenue ₹47.2975 crore versus ₹60.5001 crore in FY25 and PAT ₹2.4154 crore versus ₹6.3205 crore. The weak growth/margin comparison cautions against assuming sector demand always translates into profitable growth. Its Ind AS basis differs from Rappid's presentation; no raw multiple transplant is used.\\n\\nRappid's higher reported profitability is a positive relative to these weaker margin examples, but superior profitability without cash conversion is not demonstrated superior economics. Peer scope is operating comparison only, not a full peer governance audit or recommendation. Sources: [Kirloskar Brothers FY26](https://www.kirloskarpumps.com/virtual-annual-report-2026/), [Hawa FY25](https://hawaengltd.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/09/FINAL_Hawa_Engineers_Ltd___AR___2024-25.pdf), [Atam FY26 results](https://www.atamvalves.in/wp-content/uploads/Atam_Valves_Limited_FinancialResults_31032026.pdf), SIPO and SCALL.\\n\\n## 6. Revenue growth and scale\\n| FY | Sales | Operating EBITDA | PAT | CFO after finance | All capex | CFO less capex | Debt | Book equity |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 2021 | 7.68 | 1.47 | 0.21 | NS | NS | NS | 9.60 | -0.46 |\\n| 2022 | 12.14 | 1.95 | 0.29 | -0.32 | 0.05 | -0.38 | 9.78 | -0.25 |\\n| 2023 | 16.40 | 2.31 | 0.58 | -1.97 | 1.01 | -2.98 | 9.93 | 3.28 |\\n| 2024 | 36.51 | 7.35 | 4.13 | -2.48 | 2.72 | -5.20 | 10.98 | 12.37 |\\n| 2025 | 52.12 | 9.93 | 6.04 | -12.82 | 3.38 | -16.19 | 8.41 | 45.25 |\\n| 2026 | 53.23 | 10.31 | 6.48 | -10.83 | 1.79 | -12.62 | 17.84 | 51.71 |\\n\\n| FY | Absolute sales addition | Operating EBIT | Normalized PAT | Current-share EPS comparator | ROCE | ROE | Average AR / inventory / payable days |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 2021 | NS | 1.34 | 0.18 | 0.41 | NS | NS | NS / NS / NS |\\n| 2022 | 4.46 | 1.74 | 0.21 | 0.56 | 18.64% | NS | NS / NS / NS |\\n| 2023 | 4.26 | 2.01 | 0.42 | 1.11 | 17.65% | NS | NS / NS / NS |\\n| 2024 | 20.11 | 6.84 | 4.08 | 7.95 | 37.41% | 52.75% | 57.9 / 127.3 / 38.9 |\\n| 2025 | 15.61 | 9.15 | 6.02 | 11.63 | 23.76% | 20.96% | 96.5 / 133.1 / 35.5 |\\n| 2026 | 1.11 | 9.67 | 6.26 | 12.48 | 15.70% | 13.36% | 151.1 / 207.2 / 27.8 |\\n\\nROCE here is operating EBIT / average opening-and-closing (book equity + interest-bearing debt), including preference equity once. ROE is all-equity-owner PAT / average total book equity (including preference capital), not ordinary-equity ROE, reported only when both equity endpoints are positive. FY22 is NM for negative equity; FY23's crossing from negative equity is also NM. The FY24 preference transaction and FY25 IPO materially change these denominators, so high historical ROE is not organic shareholder compounding. The EPS column applies current 5,191,984 shares to each year's PAT attributable to ordinary shares where separately disclosed (FY24), otherwise PAT, solely as a constant-share scale comparator; it is not historical reported or weighted EPS. Average working-capital days use average opening/closing balances and current-year sales, adjusted material cost and purchases respectively. Closing and average proxies are both retained rather than presented as exact customer settlement terms.\\n\\nAll amounts above are crore. FY22 CFO is restated IPO CFO of ₹1.1376 crore **before** finance less finance cost ₹1.4616 crore, yielding negative ₹0.3240 crore on the later after-finance convention. FY23–26 CFO already includes finance cost, because the cash-flow reconciliation starts at PBT without adding it back and financing cash flow does not deduct it separately. Interest is therefore **not subtracted twice**. FY21 comparable CFO/capex is unavailable in the selected reconciled history and remains NS.\\n\\nFY21–26 nominal operating-sales CAGR is **47.27%**; FY23–26 is **48.07%**. The depressed FY21 pandemic base matters: FY20 revenue was ₹9.4209241 crore against FY21 ₹7.6830956 crore. FY19 comparative revenue was ₹4.5073996 crore. Growth was not smooth. FY26 increased just 2.13%; rounded H1 sales rose from ₹19.62 to ₹28.82 crore, while H2 fell from ₹32.51 to ₹24.41 crore. July's subsequent 28% sales-growth update is encouraging but unaudited and not a substitute for cash and margin data.\\n\\nRevenue is classified as **nominal, constant-entity organic operating-sales growth**, with medium confidence, not verified unit/volume growth. No acquired perimeter has been identified, but price, volume and mix contributions cannot be separated. Gross manufacturing value added, revenue less material/stock-adjusted cost, rose ₹9.88681 → ₹14.06742 → ₹15.492 crore across FY24–26. This provides an additional operating-scale check rather than giving full credit to gross material pass-through. It is not cash value added.\\n\\nFY26 exports increased ₹4.392 → ₹10.203 crore, while domestic sales declined ₹47.066 → ₹42.611 crore. The net unbilled-revenue movement declined ₹0.667 → ₹0.419 crore. These changes explain the approximately ₹1.1084 crore total increase, subject to statement rounding. The incorrect gross unbilled figures in Note 21 are not used to inflate sales or claim a giant contract asset. Sources: S20–S26, SIPO, SDECK, SJUL.\\n\\n## 7. Growth quality and reinvestment runway\\n| FY | Operating WC | Operating IC | NOPAT | Average-IC ROIC | ΔIC | ΔNOPAT / ΔIC | AR / inventory / payable days |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 2023 | 7.83 | 12.34 | 1.50 | NS | NS | NS | 72.8 / 215.4 / 71.0 |\\n| 2024 | 15.96 | 22.64 | 5.13 | 29.32% | 10.29 | 35.21% | 83.2 / 158.8 / 46.8 |\\n| 2025 | 35.86 | 45.11 | 6.86 | 20.25% | 22.48 | 7.71% | 134.7 / 155.1 / 37.4 |\\n| 2026 | 53.58 | 63.91 | 7.26 | 13.31% | 18.80 | 2.10% | 170.3 / 258.0 / 22.3 |\\n\\nOperating IC = net PPE + inventory + trade receivables + short-term operating advances + other current assets excluding identified tax assets − payables − other operating liabilities − operating provisions + operating security deposits. It excludes cash, bank deposits and deferred-tax assets; preference equity already within book equity is not counted again as debt. NOPAT = (PBT − other income + finance cost) ×75%, using an explicit analytical 25% tax assumption. Average-IC ROIC uses opening and closing IC; days are closing-balance proxies, not exact invoice-aging statistics. Inventory days use adjusted material cost; payable days use purchases, so the cash-cycle proxy is an approximation.\\n\\n| FY | Δ operating WC | Δ PPE | Δ security | Cash capex − P&L D&A | PPE reconciliation residual |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 2024 | 8.12523 | 2.17268 | -0.00510 | 2.20791 | -0.03523 |\\n| 2025 | 19.90308 | 2.55955 | 0.01524 | 2.59304 | -0.03349 |\\n| 2026 | 17.72473 | 1.04169 | 0.03122 | 1.15900 | -0.11731 |\\n\\n| FY | Equity + debt − cash − nonoperating investments | Tax liabilities − tax assets − DTA | Residual | Operating IC |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 2023 | 12.39741 | -0.05475 | 0.00002 | 12.34268 |\\n| 2024 | 21.59819 | 1.03729 | 0.00001 | 22.63549 |\\n| 2025 | 43.60026 | 1.51310 | 0.00000 | 45.11336 |\\n| 2026 | 62.29400 | 1.61900 | -0.00200 | 63.91100 |\\n\\nThe financing bridge subtracts reported cash (including its short deposits) and separately identified noncurrent deposits/unquoted investments, irrespective of spendability. Income-tax liabilities are added back and current tax assets/deferred-tax assets removed to match operating IC. Small remaining residuals are retained for rounding/classification, not plugged into returns. FY26's restricted-deposit treatment in valuation is a separate availability question, not an accounting reclassification.\\n\\nThe capital bridge is additive: ΔIC = Δ operating WC + Δ PPE + Δ operating security deposits. Cash capex minus P&L D&A need not equal Δ PPE: disposals, revaluation-reserve depreciation, classification and cash/noncash timing remain in the explicit residual above. FY26's asset schedule has about ₹0.6462 crore depreciation, including about ₹0.0111 crore charged to revaluation reserve, versus P&L D&A ₹0.635 crore; cash disposal proceeds are not assumed equal to disposed net book value. No residual is silently set to zero.\\n\\nThree-year incremental NOPAT/ΔIC is **11.15%**. A lagged crosscheck uses FY24–25 company-wide IC deployment against FY26 less FY23 NOPAT and gives **17.55%**. That cohort has one to two subsequent operating years, but neither machine-by-machine commissioning dates nor attributable project cash flows are available. It is a lag sensitivity, not a certified project IRR. The FY24–25 investment in machinery and working capital is therefore not dismissed as wholly unseasoned, while the more recent July 2026 equipment purchases are not assumed already commissioned. Precise five-year incremental ROIC is **NM** because early loans, advances, provisions, revaluation and restated balance-sheet classifications cannot be bridged reliably enough. Revenue CAGR does not require that missing IC precision.\\n\\nThe near-term runway is tangible but capital hungry. The ₹18.0576 crore BHEL five-ship order (excluding taxes) places three shipsets around a year and two into FY27–28. The ₹8.55 crore Shree order converted a prior LOI into a formal order; it is not counted twice. July disclosed ₹40 crore firm orders plus ₹11 crore customer confirmations awaiting POs; the latter are not firm backlog. The three large Q1 orders totaling approximately ₹29.85 crore are inside that book, not additive. August's ₹2.84 crore L&T order requires delivery ten weeks **after drawing/QAP approval**, not ten weeks from the announcement. September's ₹54.36 crore backlog is the later snapshot, including ₹0.8531 crore HSL and ₹1.56 crore Solas orders due by March 2027.\\n\\nThe September US update is product/factory-specific final qualification testing, not completed UL approval, exclusivity or commercial purchase orders. The undisclosed prospective customer cannot be modeled as assured revenue. The February **2025** announcement targeting 50% volume CAGR in FY26/FY27 and capex completion in June 2025 must be dated correctly. FY26 delivery fell short; June 2026 procurement of ₹1.25 crore equipment was expected within 25 days, while July still described POs/advances. Neither a ₹120 crore mix-dependent capacity claim nor six test benches establishes audited throughput or utilization. Sources: SCALL, SPROMISE, SJUL, SBHEL, SSHREE, SAUG, SSEP, SUS.\\n\\n## 8. Management, governance and execution\\nThe September 30 shareholding filing, submitted October 12, reports promoter-group shares 2,685,736 of 5,191,984, reported 51.73%; public shareholding is reported 48.27%. It explicitly reports no pledge, non-disposal undertaking or other encumbrance. Promoter lock-in is 1,100,000 shares, or reported **40.96% of promoter shares**, not 40.96% of the entire company. Lock-in is not pledge. The April FY26 declaration of no newly created encumbrance is a different period/test and is not used as a substitute for September's direct filing. [Primary September filing](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/xbrl/SHP_1736091_12102026034610_WEB.xml).\\n\\nIPO gross proceeds were ₹30.40956 crore, from 1,369,800 shares at ₹222. The original objects included debt repayment, capex/renovation, acquisition and general corporate purposes. The FY26 report shows capex ₹3.086 crore utilized against the ₹6.731 crore object, leaving approximately ₹3.6451 crore; ₹4 crore acquisition money remained unused. The ₹7.6451 crore variation to working capital was approved through the April 2026 shareholder process. The contemporaneous voting record points to April 29 results disclosed April 30, not an assumed April 17 approval merely because Note 32 says EGM April 17. Existing deposits are smaller than the unutilized-object total and are not automatically legally or operationally available cash.\\n\\nThis is capital-allocation drift: an acquisition/capacity thesis became working-capital funding. Approval mitigates unauthorized-use concerns but does not prove attractive returns. The pre-IPO ₹4.98 crore convertible-preference transaction and September 2024 IPO must not be redated to 2025 based on erroneous Note 3 narrative. Verified ordinary shares remain 5,191,984 at the October market date. The model prohibits pre-entry equity issuance rather than rewriting that observed share count.\\n\\nRelated-party scrutiny is material. The IPO restatement shows FY24 sales to Vinpra of ₹7.657 crore, approximately 20.97% of FY24 revenue, versus much smaller earlier amounts. A director's prior resignation does not alone establish the later absence of influence; omission from the latest RPT list does not settle current customer/relationship status. FY24 Vinpra closing receivables were ₹1.5893 crore; the schedule's credits are not automatically bank receipts. Obtain current beneficial relationship, balances, collection and arm's-length terms. FY22–26 disclosed remuneration/professional fees/rent are retained in the evidence file, not only the latest year. FY26 disclosed Gaurav remuneration ₹0.62 crore, Vijay ₹0.12 crore, Mansi ₹0.28 crore and rent ₹0.026 crore, plus independent-director amounts. Evaluate these against profits and liquidity, not as automatic abuse.\\n\\nExecution positives include export expansion and formal marine orders; negatives include missed growth/capex timing, the unused acquisition allocation and repeated filing corrections. June 2025 correspondence acknowledged a missing unmodified-opinion declaration/format questions; January 2026 clarified the single operating segment. Those are disclosure weaknesses, not evidence that the company admitted a profit restatement or fraud. Sources: SIPO, S26, SOBJECT, SCL25, SCL26, SSHP26, audit_evidence.json.\\n\\n## 9. Financial forensics\\n### Audit chronology, including annexures\\n- **FY19 bundle:** its appended signed main/IFC report is explicitly for FY18, dated September 5, 2018, by Amrut Deshmukh. It cannot be counted as FY19 audit assurance.\\n- **FY20:** signed Parab & Kadam opinion is unmodified, October 5, 2020, although directors' text names another firm. CARO says inventory procedures were reasonable but **not adequate** and records historical PF/ESIC/professional-tax arrears beyond six months. No bank default is reported.\\n- **FY21:** Kalantri Assawa's unmodified opinion is dated October 30, 2021. The main report calls CARO inapplicable, yet an annexure contains irregular statutory deposits/old arrears. A remuneration row is incomplete boilerplate. No fraud/bank default is reported.\\n- **FY22:** Vijay Bhardwaj's September 26, 2022 opinion is unmodified; CARO is stated inapplicable and no separate positive IFC conclusion is inferred.\\n- **FY23:** Vijay Bhardwaj's September 6, 2023 opinion is unmodified; CARO is stated inapplicable. The incomplete IFC sentence is not affirmative assurance.\\n- **FY24:** KAVA's June 12, 2024 main opinion is unmodified, but the India daily-backup exception appears in the books/legal reporting. TDS ₹14,37,671 and PF ₹19,454 were overdue beyond six months. IFC exemption language is not a positive IFC audit opinion.\\n- **FY25:** KAVA's main opinion is unmodified; the India-backup exception repeats. Internal-audit and private-company remuneration language conflict with listed-company/corporate disclosures. Read the appended IFC conclusion separately.\\n- **FY26:** KAVA/Vivek Jalan's May 27, 2026 main opinion is unmodified, with adequate IFC opinion and a separate positive audit-trail statement. The daily India-backup exception nevertheless remains. CARO says there is no internal-audit system while corporate information names an internal auditor. It reports no fraud, bank default or auditor resignation, and no identified one-year material uncertainty. These are not a guarantee of future liquidity. Later no-over-six-month-dues wording does not establish payment dates of older arrears.\\n\\n### Accounting and cash reconciliation\\nReported FY26 EPS of 0.1 on the P&L face is a scale error; PAT divided by verified shares gives about ₹12.48, consistent with Note 30's approximate ₹12.5. FY25's 5,192 thousand denominator appears to use year-end shares despite midyear IPO issuance, so a true weighted-average comparative EPS is not reconstructed without a share-date calculation. The FY25 common-share comparator of about ₹11.63 is expressly a **year-end-share** measure, not a claim that the printed weighted EPS is reliable.\\n\\nFY26 Note 2.6 prospectively revises useful-life/residual estimates; the benefit is not quantified. H2 D&A of roughly ₹0.12 crore versus H1 ₹0.52 crore heightens normalization uncertainty. Operating-only normalized PAT at a 25% analytical tax rate is ₹6.25875 crore. An additional ₹0.15 crore pretax D&A sensitivity reduces it to ₹6.14625 crore. This is a stress, not an invented official estimate adjustment.\\n\\nThe Note 21 gross unbilled opening/closing figures (₹5,267.8/₹5,309.7 lakh) conflict with Note 18's ₹119.4/₹161.3 lakh. The net increase ₹41.9 lakh agrees. Use the balance-sheet amounts; retain the gross-note error. Generic no-contingency wording does not erase the explicitly disclosed ₹0.146 crore bank guarantee. IPO tax proceedings and later generic nonmaterial-dispute language do not prove settlement; current closure documents are ND. The printed DSCR has denominator/unit problems and is not adopted; independently computed operating EBIT/finance is used instead.\\n\\nFY26 cash flow reconciles PBT ₹8.664 + D&A ₹0.635 − long-term provisions ₹0.030 − the presentation's short-term-provision/tax adjustment ₹2.108 − other income ₹0.319 − inventory ₹10.505 − receivables ₹5.599 − advances ₹0.010 − other assets ₹0.162 − payables ₹1.352 + other liabilities ₹0.460 − tax paid ₹0.500 = **−₹10.826 crore**. The short-term provision line embeds current-tax adjustment; advance tax and tax-paid presentation are not deducted twice. CFO −₹10.826, investing −₹5.589 and financing +₹9.428 reconcile cash from ₹9.494 to ₹2.507 crore. That closing cash number includes ₹2.120 crore sub-12-month deposits; cash/current accounts are only ₹0.387 crore. Another ₹4.756 crore deposits are longer term.\\n\\nThree-year CFO/PAT is approximately **−157%**. FY26 inventory increased ₹10.50486 crore and AR ₹5.59962 crore; payable reduction also consumed funding. This is not explained away by PAT growth or listing proceeds. Deposits, IPO object balances, tax liabilities and overdrafts must be reconciled through bank evidence before using an accounting net-cash figure as spendable liquidity.\\n\\n### Historical vintages retained, not spliced invisibly\\nIPO-restated FY22 PAT ₹0.2898 differs from original ₹0.3243699. FY23 original PAT ₹0.5759394, later comparative ₹0.57578 and IPO-restated ₹0.4556 are distinct vintages; original AR/payable/other-asset classifications also differ materially. The selected later-comparative basis is identified in historical_inputs.json, alongside every alternative.\\n\\nFY24 IPO-restated CFO −₹1.0733 crore is before finance ₹1.4029; after finance it is −₹2.4762, versus later-comparative −₹2.48153, residual −₹0.00533 crore. This reconciles most of the apparent CFO discrepancy but does **not** resolve all cash/equity restatement differences: IPO cash ₹1.5830 includes deposits versus later cash ₹1.22293; equity ₹12.2198 versus ₹12.36796. The original FY24 CFO rounding difference of ₹0.00007 crore is separately immaterial. Restatement auditor Vinay Bhushan relied on prior auditors; it does not create identical accounting vintages. All audit observations, RPT history and reconciliation limitations are retained in the standalone inputs. Sources: S19–S26, SN26, SIPO.\\n\\n## 10. Valuation and market expectations\\nCurrent reported P/E is **28.93×**, operating-only normalized P/E **29.95×**, and D&A-stress P/E **30.50×**. EV/operating EBITDA is **19.87×**, using only ₹0.387 crore freely available cash proxy against debt, not assuming all deposits free. EV is October market capitalization combined with the March 31 audited balance-sheet anchor, not a verified October net-debt figure. These are descriptive multiples, not supported fair-value targets. Deposit accessibility can lower accounting net debt but cannot repair cash timing after a prior breach.\\n\\n### Linked cash model and financing bounds\\nThe model starts March 31 with ₹0.387 crore cash, ₹6.876 crore deposits, ₹17.843 crore debt, ₹24.832 crore AR, ₹26.675 crore inventory, ₹2.767 crore payables, ₹4.843 crore other net operating WC and ₹10.278 crore PPE. The 203-day pre-entry bridge includes a distinct April 29 interval **before** an optional April 30 deposit release. A separate, clearly unverified alternative assumes lawful availability of existing deposits at the March anchor. Neither alternative creates new IPO cash.\\n\\nPre-entry growth rates are 0/12/25% bear/base/bull, a ₹1.25 crore equipment cash allocation, ₹1.901 crore old tax settlement in June, a ₹0.25 crore cash floor, 11% debt interest and 4% deposit income. Tax timing and monthly growth are assumptions, not bank observations. Growth capex is spread across the bridge rather than falsely asserting an exact historical payment date. The forward base begins from an annualized bridge run-rate of about ₹59.62 crore, then grows to about ₹66.78 crore in its first full forward year; this is a transparent run-rate convention, not a reported FY27 forecast.\\n\\nEach period separately models customer receipts = revenue − ΔAR; supplier payments = purchased-material cost + Δinventory − Δpayables; payroll/other opex; other WC; current cash taxes; maintenance/growth capex; interest; old taxes; loss replenishment; principal; and remaining customer receipts. Fifty percent of receipts precede payments in the central timing assumption. Gross outflow-first, 25% and 100% front-loaded receipts are separate sensitivities. This is a bounded monthly settlement-cycle stress, not a claim to know daily invoice dates; additional within-month clustering remains a risk.\\n\\nBase forward assumptions: 12% annual sales growth, 18% EBITDA margin, AR 170 days, inventory 240 purchased-material days, payable 25 days, other net WC 9% of sales and PPE requirement 20% of sales. Bear uses flat sales, 14% margin, AR190/inventory280/payable20, plus 10% opening AR/inventory loss and guarantee call. Bull uses 25% growth, 21% margin and improved AR140/inventory190/payable35. The denominator for modeled inventory is material purchases, not the historical adjusted-COGS day ratio. All assumptions and alternatives are explicit, not optimized to force a target.\\n\\nMaintenance capex is 1.2× modeled D&A, with 1.0× and 1.5× sensitivities; D&A is 7% of rolling PPE. Growth capex is the incremental required fixed-asset base, while maintenance above D&A also accumulates PPE. Debt principal is repaid in month 60 in base/bull and month 12 in bear; a month-1 no-renewal stress is included. No new bank line or perpetual refinancing is assumed.\\n\\nOptional equity is allowed **only after entry**, only if the bridge has not failed, at ₹288.80 (20% below entry), 2% issuance costs, gross cap ₹15 crore, maximum 1,000,000 new shares and authorized ceiling 10,172,000 shares. Required shares are rounded **up** to a whole share, capacity rounded down. The ₹15 crore limit binds before the one-million-share ceiling. Just-in-time completion is deliberately optimistic and **uncommitted**. A failed bridge cannot be rescued retrospectively. No ordinary IRR is emitted after a cash-floor breach, even if later cash becomes positive.\\n\\n| Path | First breach (scenario event) | Minimum cash | Equity gross raised | Final shares | Ordinary / conditional IRR |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| bear_none | 2026-04-29 supplier_settlement_including_inventory_and_payables | -31.37 | 0.00 | 5191984 | NS / NS |\\n| base_none | 2026-04-29 supplier_settlement_including_inventory_and_payables | -33.77 | 0.00 | 5191984 | NS / NS |\\n| bull_none | 2026-04-29 supplier_settlement_including_inventory_and_payables | -51.76 | 0.00 | 5191984 | NS / NS |\\n| bear_bounded_equity_conditional_anchor_access | 2027-10-20 principal_repayment_no_automatic_rollover | -10.10 | 15.00 | 5711374 | NS / NS |\\n| base_bounded_equity_conditional_anchor_access | 2031-10-20 principal_repayment_no_automatic_rollover | -13.32 | 15.00 | 5711374 | NS / NS |\\n| bull_bounded_equity_conditional_anchor_access | 2026-08-31 current_period_cash_tax | -46.01 | 0.00 | 5191984 | NS / NS |\\n\\nBase no-financing annual trajectory below is an **infeasible diagnostic**, not an investable forecast. Continuing its arithmetic retains the failure and quantifies funding demand; negative cash is never presented as a valid financing balance.\\n\\n| Year to October | Revenue | EBITDA | Interest | Tax | ΔWC | Maintenance | Growth capex | Principal | Closing cash |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 2027 | 66.78 | 12.02 | 1.96 | 2.30 | 8.27 | 1.05 | 1.74 | 0.00 | -6.06 |\\n| 2028 | 74.79 | 13.46 | 1.96 | 2.63 | 7.85 | 1.20 | 1.43 | 0.00 | -7.46 |\\n| 2029 | 83.76 | 15.08 | 1.96 | 3.00 | 8.79 | 1.34 | 1.60 | 0.00 | -8.87 |\\n| 2030 | 93.81 | 16.89 | 1.96 | 3.42 | 9.85 | 1.50 | 1.79 | 0.00 | -10.30 |\\n| 2031 | 105.07 | 18.91 | 1.96 | 3.89 | 11.03 | 1.68 | 2.00 | 17.84 | -29.59 |\\n\\n### Earned terminal valuation, not arbitrary exit P/E\\nFor every path, a further 12-month gross-cash continuation is calculated with no new financing. Forward NOPAT + D&A − maintenance − growth capex − incremental WC yields earned FCFF. EV = FCFF/(discount rate − terminal growth), only for positive FCFF. The central rate/growth is 14%/3%, with 12/14/16% ×2/3/4% grid. An explicit operating reserve covers the most negative cumulative intra-period cash draw plus the floor; only cash **above that reserve** is excess cash. Terminal equity = EV + excess cash − remaining debt, divided by fully diluted shares. Restricted deposits are excluded, with a separately marked recoverability sensitivity. A terminal cash breach, negative recurring continuation cash or nonpositive FCFF disqualifies returns.\\n\\nForward operating/incremental returns, reinvestment, terminal reserve, all cash events and shares are retained for every candidate. Entry DCF is an unlevered diagnostic before future dilution and never treats later issue proceeds as undiluted value accruing to existing shareholders. No terminal multiple is awarded merely because a company has survived five years.\\n\\nBest among all retained feasible sensitivity paths (not a supported base): p_489428e1605013eaed33, terminal price ₹417.58, conditional wealth 1.1567×, conditional annual return 2.95%.\\n\\nThis conditional path assumes 25% forward growth, 22% EBITDA margin, 120-day AR, 150-day purchased-material inventory, 40-day payables, other net WC of 7% of sales, PPE intensity of 18%, full unverified anchor-deposit access, and optional bounded equity. Its entry shares remain 5191984, final shares 5434052, equity gross raised ₹6.99 crore, and post-entry dilution 4.45%. The 12% pre-entry bridge-growth assumption remains distinct from forward growth.\\n\\n| Discount rate | Terminal growth | Conditional terminal price | Conditional IRR |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 12.00% | 2.00% | 480.35 | 5.88% |\\n| 12.00% | 3.00% | 509.80 | 7.15% |\\n| 12.00% | 4.00% | 546.68 | 8.65% |\\n| 14.00% | 2.00% | 400.77 | 2.11% |\\n| 14.00% | 3.00% | 417.58 | 2.95% |\\n| 14.00% | 4.00% | 437.81 | 3.93% |\\n| 16.00% | 2.00% | 343.93 | -0.96% |\\n| 16.00% | 3.00% | 353.74 | -0.41% |\\n| 16.00% | 4.00% | 365.23 | 0.23% |\\n\\nThe following ceilings discount that **best sampled conditional** price, not a supported base price. They are sensitivity outputs, **not buy levels**, because the deposit bridge, implementation and earnings-quality gates remain unresolved. A lower market quote cannot itself solve an issuer funding gap.\\n\\n| Required 5Y annual return | Entry ceiling from best sampled conditional price | Wealth hurdle |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| 12.00% | 236.95 | 1.76234 |\\n| 15.00% | 207.61 | 2.01136 |\\n| 18.00% | 182.53 | 2.28776 |\\n\\n### Reverse expectations and two-variable sensitivity\\n| Economics | Financing | Feasible sampled growth | Maximum sampled wealth | Search result |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| unchanged_economics | none | 0% | 0.3336 | No adjacent feasible bracket at 12/15/18% IRR, 5× or 10× |\\n| unchanged_economics | bounded_equity | 0%, 5% | 0.3382 | No adjacent feasible bracket at 12/15/18% IRR, 5× or 10× |\\n| cash_turnaround_counterfactual | none | 0%, 5%, 10%, 15%, 20% | 1.0326 | No adjacent feasible bracket at 12/15/18% IRR, 5× or 10× |\\n| cash_turnaround_counterfactual | bounded_equity | 0%, 5%, 10%, 15%, 20%, 25% | 1.1567 | No adjacent feasible bracket at 12/15/18% IRR, 5× or 10× |\\n\\nEach of four regime/funding combinations tests 0–60% forward sales growth in 5-point steps, holding the bridge separately at the base pre-growth assumption. Targets are 12%,15%,18% annual return, 5× and 10× wealth. Only adjacent cash-feasible samples can bracket bisection; every failed grid/root candidate retains its complete cash path. No extrapolation crosses a funding failure. The result is **no feasible adjacent bracket in these bounded sampled grids**, not mathematical proof that every nonmonotonic root is absent. The 22% margin/AR120/inventory150/payable40 turnaround is explicitly a counterfactual, not guidance.\\n\\nTwo-way table: terminal price / conditional IRR with 12% forward growth, optional bounded equity and full **unverified** anchor-deposit access. NS cells failed cash/continuation tests; they are not assigned fictional IRRs.\\n\\n| EBITDA margin / AR days | 120 | 150 | 170 | 200 |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 16.00% | NS: cash failure | NS: cash failure | NS: cash failure | NS: cash failure |\\n| 18.00% | 163.67 / -14.63% | NS: cash failure | NS: cash failure | NS: cash failure |\\n| 20.00% | 198.33 / -11.29% | NS: cash failure | NS: cash failure | NS: cash failure |\\n| 22.00% | 236.22 / -8.13% | 218.81 / -9.53% | 208.24 / -10.42% | NS: cash failure |\\n\\nAll 97 distinct normalized paths, their 9 terminal valuations, test aliases and reverse-attempt aliases are retained losslessly. Identical assumptions share one complete path, not duplicate megabytes; a failed path is never deleted to make the search appear successful. Sources: MODEL assumptions, S26, SCALL, SOBJECT; model_engine.py and model_inputs.json.\\n\\n## 11. Bear case and permanent-loss channels\\nThe central risk chain is slower customer approvals/collections plus inventory buildup, followed by metal or fixed-price margin pressure, overdraft dependence, tax/supplier payments and debt nonrenewal. The equity holder can lose through both business impairment and financing dilution. Positive EBITDA does not pay a supplier before cash arrives. A book-equity figure backed by specialized stock and slow receivables is not a liquidation floor.\\n\\nThe explicit bear links 10% opening AR impairment, 10% inventory loss/replenishment, the disclosed guarantee call, weaker margin and early principal runoff. Even conditional opening deposit access plus the nearly ₹15 crore modeled issue does not survive its principal payment. Cash failure yields **NS ordinary and conditional IRR**, not a spurious bounded negative return. Recovery value after an insolvency or restructuring is ND; no arbitrary terminal P/E is applied to a failed going concern.\\n\\nOther loss channels are customer concentration, unbilled/billing disputes, supplier terms, repeated report-control weaknesses, capex yielding less usable capacity than claimed, delayed US approval and inability to execute a 600-share lot at reasonable participation. These are risks, not accusations of fraud or statements that a future default is certain. No lending covenant headroom or committed refinancing has been verified.\\n\\n## 12. 5× / 10× potential\\nFivefold wealth in five years requires about 37.97% annual return; tenfold requires about 58.49%. At ₹361 these require at least ₹1,805/₹3,610 per fully diluted share at the horizon, absent dividends. Required equity value rises further with new shares. Multiplying today's shares by a hypothetical target while ignoring issuance would understate the hurdle.\\n\\nNeither target is bracketed by adjacent cash-feasible candidates in the bounded reverse grids. Higher growth absorbs more working capital and fixed investment, which can exhaust finance before the terminal business reaches the proposed size. Commercial capacity/order opportunity is not equivalent to funded shareholder compounding. The thesis would need demonstrated collections, lower working-capital intensity, attractive **incremental** capital returns, disciplined capex, dependable financing and sustained operating margins, not simply an optimistic exit multiple. A 5×/10× claim is therefore unsupported at this price and evidence set, rather than declared mathematically impossible for all future outcomes.\\n\\n## 13. Monitoring checklist and falsification tests\\n1. Obtain March-to-current bank/deposit statements or a credible filed reconciliation: lien, maturity, IPO-object restrictions, withdrawal authority and actual deployment. Reconcile every release against cash and the shareholder approval date.\\n2. Obtain September/next-half financials and cash flow, not only sales or order announcements. Track receivable aging, subsequent receipts, inventory aging/held dispatch and customer advances. Positive CFO with reduced inventory/AR intensity is the key improvement; mere PAT growth is insufficient.\\n3. Reconcile overdraft sanctioned/utilized limits, maturity/renewal, covenant terms, security and bank guarantees. A committed new line must be evidenced before entering the committed-finance case.\\n4. Reconcile the IPO utilization statement, capex completion/commissioning, acquisition cancellation and working-capital variation. Match equipment invoices, delivery, installation and throughput rather than treating POs as completion.\\n5. Ask for quantified useful-life-change impact, corrected EPS/share chronology, unbilled note, DSCR, comparative financial vintages and an explanation of the internal-audit contradiction and India-backup remedy.\\n6. Follow Vinpra's relationship status, current sales/balances and collection terms; monitor promoter remuneration against cash generation. Maintain dated pledge/encumbrance checks without confusing lock-in with pledge.\\n7. Reconcile backlog opening + new firm orders − recognized/delivered orders − cancellations = closing. Keep LOIs, customer confirmations and US qualification separate from formal orders. Verify L&T approval dates and BHEL delivery milestones.\\n8. Before any trade, verify live bid/offer depth, active series, lot, circuit and ASM/GSM/ESM/trade-to-trade status. The static October security master is not a dated all-clear for surveillance.\\n\\nA falsified cash thesis would be sustained cash collections and profitable deliveries without recurring external equity, supported by a full capital bridge. A falsified moat thesis would be repeated repricing inability, lost approvals or margin compression despite backlog. A price decline alone falsifies neither.\\n\\n## 14. Final classification and conditional action\\n**Hold — unresolved / research only. BQS 4.50; GQS 5.00 after cap; IAS NS (55% coverage, bounded 2.375–6.425).** This is not an existing-position instruction and not a personalized allocation recommendation. It is an evidence-based classification as of the stated cutoffs.\\n\\nLiquidity is investability, not just turnover. The official 60-session sample (July24–October19) contains 59 traded sessions and one absent issuer row on September4 in an otherwise complete exchange archive, correctly counted as zero. Mean turnover is ₹4,035,899.50; median ₹1,955,640, the lower denominator. A ₹5 lakh reference exit requires 5.113415557 sessions at 5%, versus 12.78353889 at 2% and 2.556707779 at 10%. These are continuous arithmetic estimates, not executable promises. At volume-based 5% participation, only 13 of the historical 60 sessions permitted even one whole 600-share lot (3 at 2%, 33 at 10%). This is a historical capacity count, not a future exit schedule. A 600-share lot costs ₹216,600; two lots cost ₹433,200, three ₹649,800, so ₹5 lakh cannot be implemented exactly. One lot exceeds the ₹97,782 median daily 5% allowance. The 20-session mean/median are ₹4,076,001/₹2,254,575. There is no claim that the master's market-maker-spread field is a live spread. Its ₹288.80–₹433.20 band indicates a 20% static price band, not verified current restriction clearance.\\n\\nDo not upgrade to investment consideration until the funding bridge and data questions are resolved and a cash-feasible base case clears the desired return with realistic dilution and lot execution. Any conditional price ceiling above is subordinate to those gates. No purchase, sale or publication is authorized by this report alone.\\n\\n## 15. Sources, assumptions and open questions\\nSource hierarchy: signed audited statements/notes and exchange filings first; contemporaneous management call/deck/order announcements second; economic interpretation and model assumptions separately labeled. Aggregate official market/shareholding extracts are retained with source hashes. Source bodies, copyrighted PDFs/OCR, raw shareholder personal records, private service identifiers, transfer URLs and helper code are excluded from deliverables.\\n\\n| ID | Primary document / locator |\\n| --- | --- |\\n| S26 | [FY2026 issuer annual report](https://www.rappidvalves.in/assets/img/uploads/annual_report/3c0d8-annual-report-2025-26.pdf); Printed pages 54–69 notice; 90–105 main audit, CARO and IFC; 106–139 accounts and notes. Issuer PDF uses double-page spreads. |\\n| S25 | [FY2025 issuer annual report](https://www.rappidvalves.in/assets/img/uploads/annual_report/b1782-annual-report-2024-25.pdf); Printed pages 71–79 main audit and annexures; 80–108 accounts and notes (physical pages 86–114). |\\n| S24 | [FY2024 issuer annual report](https://www.rappidvalves.in/assets/img/uploads/annual_report/88dc0-annual-report_2023-24.pdf); Physical pages 1 and 4–8 main audit/CARO; 9–19 financial statements and notes. |\\n| S23 | [FY2023 issuer annual report](https://www.rappidvalves.in/assets/img/uploads/annual_report/d92a7-annual-report-2022-23.pdf); Physical pages 10–11 signed audit; 12–22 accounts and notes. No separate positive CARO/IFC conclusion. |\\n| S22 | [FY2022 issuer annual report](https://www.rappidvalves.in/assets/img/uploads/annual_report/47b2b-annual-report-2021-22.pdf); Physical pages 10–12 signed audit; 13–23 financials, including FY21 comparative. |\\n| S21 | [FY2021 issuer annual report](https://www.rappidvalves.in/assets/img/uploads/annual_report/0ad71-annual-report_2020-21.pdf); Physical pages 16–20 audit and annexure; 21–41 accounts and notes. |\\n| S20 | [FY2020 issuer annual report](https://www.rappidvalves.in/assets/img/uploads/annual_report/0c6f8-annual-report_2019-20.pdf); Physical pages 5–10 signed audit and annexure; 11–18 financial statements. |\\n| S19 | [FY2019 issuer annual report](https://www.rappidvalves.in/assets/img/uploads/annual_report/9af51-annual-report_2018-19.pdf); Appended signed opinion concerns FY18, not FY19. Historical financial-vintage gap retained. |\\n| SN26 | [FY26 independent exchange annual report](https://archives.nseindia.com/annual_reports/SME_AR_31041_RAPPID_2025_2026_A_59306041_01092026201328.pdf); Same statutory report, independent exchange copy; audited accounts and audit annexures |\\n| SIPO | [Final September2024 prospectus](https://www.rappidvalves.in/assets/img/uploads/initial_public_offer/dd504-prospectus_rappid.pdf); Risk factors and customer concentration; capital history; IPO objects; F1–F13 restatement and accounting basis; F29 related-party schedule; management discussion. |\\n| SCALL | [June1 earnings call](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/RAPPID_03062026163806_Rappid_Transcript_Signed.pdf); entire call; cost escalation, held dispatch, guidance, equipment, capacity and liquidity |\\n| SDECK | [June1 investor presentation](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/RAPPID_01062026141424_Exchange_PresentationSigned.pdf); Pages 10–32: products, certifications, customer mix, capacity and half-year P&L. |\\n| SJUL | [RevisedQ1FY27 business update](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/RAPPID_10072026150526_GBU_Rappid_Signed.pdf); All six pages: correction from revenue to sales; firm backlog 40 crore versus customer confirmations of 11 crore pending purchase orders. |\\n| SSEP | [September21 orderandbacklog filing](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/RAPPID_21092026153828_Work_Order.pdf); All three pages and order annexures; firm backlog 54.36 crore. |\\n| SAUG | [L&T order filing](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/RAPPID_13082026191053_Workorder.pdf); Both pages: ten-week delivery starts after drawing and QAP approval. |\\n| SBHEL | [BHEL FleetSupportShip order](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/RAPPID_15062026102327_Work_Order.pdf); Dated June 13, exchange filing June 15: 18.0576 crore, three shipsets around one year and two in FY27–28. |\\n| SSHREE | [Shree order formalization](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/RAPPID_08062026151443_Work_Order_Shree_.pdf); Formal 8.55 crore order, approximately one-year delivery; supersedes LOI status in June 1 call. |\\n| SPLEDGE | [FY26 annual encumbrance declaration](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/team_sandeshc_29042026124914_40.pdf); April 6 declaration says no encumbrance created during FY26. It is not a September 30 zero-balance statement. |\\n| SCL26 | [January2026 financial filing clarification](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/RAPPID_19012026164650_Clarification_Letter_Signed.pdf); Single business segment; reporting omission acknowledged. Subject says quarter but the clarification concerns the September half-year. |\\n| SCL25 | [June2025 financial filing clarification](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/RAPPID_25062025164758_Claritifcationletterrappidsd.pdf); June 25 signed response admits omitted unmodified-opinion declaration; disputes or seeks guidance on half-year/balancing-figure format queries. |\\n| SPROMISE | [February2025 business guidance](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/RAPPID_01022025120242_RappidQIA.pdf); February 2025 guidance: 50% volume CAGR target for FY26/FY27, foundry talks, and June 2025 capex completion expectation. |\\n| SOBJECT | [IPO objects variation vote](https://www.rappidvalves.in/assets/img/uploads/announcement/16d56-change-in-ipo-object_scrutinizer-result.pdf); All pages: March 31–April 29 postal ballot, April 30 result; inconsistent with annual-report April 17 EGM description. |\\n| SUS | [US qualification status](https://www.rappidvalves.in/assets/img/uploads/announcement/cc2e2-strategic-business-update_rappid_sd.pdf); All four pages: final sampling/testing for product/factory approval; commercial orders not yet assured. |\\n| SSH | [NSE shareholding-pattern table](https://www.nseindia.com/companies-listing/corporate-filings-shareholding-pattern); NSE SME table filtered for RAPPID: September 30 promoter 51.73%, public 48.27%, filed October 12. Earlier failed XML retrieval is superseded by SSHP26. |\\n| SSHXML | [NSE linked September2026 XBRL](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/xbrl/SHP_1736091_12102026034610_WEB.xml); Same primary September XBRL as SSHP26; aggregate contents subsequently verified. No personal shareholder rows retained. |\\n| SMKT | [NSE October19 security master](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/content/cm/interop/NSE_CM_security_19102026.csv.gz); Issuer-only ISIN row. All 60 daily source URLs, complete archive hashes and issuer fields are in market_extract.json. |\\n| SKBL | [KirloskarBrothersFY26 integrated report](https://www.kirloskarpumps.com/virtual-annual-report-2026/); Manufacturing, R&D, distribution and valve-product breadth; qualitative economic benchmark only. |\\n| SHAWA | [HawaEngineersFY25annualreport](https://hawaengltd.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/09/FINAL_Hawa_Engineers_Ltd___AR___2024-25.pdf); Page 28: FY25 revenue 121.0618 crore, EBITDA including other income 6.2397, finance 2.9774, PAT 1.5958. Historical FY25, not current FY26. |\\n| SSHP26 | [NSE September 2026 shareholding XBRL, aggregate facts only](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/xbrl/SHP_1736091_12102026034610_WEB.xml); Quarter ended 30 September 2026; aggregate promoter, encumbrance and lock-in fields |\\n| SATAM | [Atam Valves FY2026 audited standalone results](https://www.atamvalves.in/wp-content/uploads/Atam_Valves_Limited_FinancialResults_31032026.pdf); Physical pages 3–5 P&L, balance sheet and cash flow; limited economic comparison, not full peer audit |\\n\\nThe source register retains document URLs, page/section locators, vintages, and hashes where bytes were obtained. Historical inputs preserve selected, original and restated values rather than replacing conflicts. Full audit observations and related-party amounts are in audit_evidence.json; no source's instructions are treated as authority. The original v3 framework is identified by title and SHA256 in framework_provenance.json, not redistributed.\\n\\nPrincipal unresolved inputs: current cash/deposit access; restrictions and true available overdraft; actual payment/collection calendar; quantitative useful-life benefit; clean comparable historical IC; project commissioning/returns; Vinpra's current relationship; backlog cancellations/margins; current live market restrictions and depth. No current fact is asserted from a post-cutoff document merely because it was easy to retrieve.\\n\\nReconstruction: Python 3.11+ standard library, build_research.py, then verify_research.py. The compact package contains **all** required inputs and code; the full package additionally contains every generated cash path, valuation and attestation. Generated outputs and attestations can be deleted and regenerated without network, raw source files, another company's package or private helpers. Exact input/output hashes and independent checks accompany the deliverables. This is research, not assurance, legal advice, or a promise of returns.\\n\"","\"number\"","\"content_markdown\"","\"Executive verdict\"","\"**Hold — unresolved. Research/watchlist only, not eligible for investment consideration on present evidence.** The issuer is Rappid Valves (India) Limited, NSE symbol RAPPID, ISIN INE0MVO01012, not a similarly named valve business. At ₹361 on 19 October 2026, 5,191,984 shares imply equity value ₹187.4306224 crore. This is an SME security with an indivisible 600-share lot.\\n\\nThe commercial story is credible enough to investigate: nominal sales expanded substantially, exports increased, and a September 2026 firm backlog of ₹54.36 crore supports marine demand. But profitable expansion has consumed, rather than generated, cash. FY26 PAT was ₹6.478 crore against CFO of negative ₹10.826 crore. Inventory and receivables absorbed far more capital than the year's incremental sales. A high reported growth history is not sufficient evidence of a self-funding compounder.\\n\\nThe most important unresolved question is not a terminal P/E. It is how existing deposits, customer collections, overdrafts, taxes and supplier payments bridge the audited March balance sheet into the October entry date, then fund deliveries without an assumed rescue. The primary gross-payment model breaches its cash floor before entry. This is an analytical failure to establish funding, **not an assertion that the company actually defaulted in April**. Ordinary return and price-expectation factors therefore remain NS. Even optimistic, explicitly conditional deposit-access and bounded-equity alternatives do not support the required return in the sampled reverse grids.\\n\\n\"","\"Research header, gates and data confidence\"","\"Research cutoff: **20 October 2026**. Market cutoff: **19 October 2026**. Sources retrieved on 23 October are used only for documents available by the cutoff. No later financial performance is admitted. Ranking eligibility is false while the data and funding gates remain unresolved. Financial basis: standalone Indian accounting reports; FY26 is the latest complete audited year available in this record. July's ₹14.87 crore update is unaudited **sales**, not a filed quarterly revenue-from-operations result. No H1FY27 financial statement is silently synthesized from it.\\n\\n| Gate | Status | Reason |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| G1 | Conditional | Reporting/stewardship weaknesses are evidenced, but not abuse sufficient to establish Fail. |\\n| G2 | Hold — unresolved | Material note, vintage and current cash-availability questions remain unresolved. |\\n| G3 | Hold — unresolved | Scenario failure does not prove actual insolvency; the current cash/deposit/bank bridge is not verified. |\\n| G4 | Conditional | Trade and lot data are verified; lot indivisibility, spread and current restriction gaps limit implementation assurance. |\\n\\nConfidence is high in the exchange price/share/lot/tape extract and latest audited statement arithmetic, medium in economic interpretation, and low in current deposit availability, bank renewal, collection timing and forward returns. Missing facts are ND (not disclosed/verified); an unscoreable factor is NS; a denominator or historical comparison that is not meaningful is NM. These labels are not zeros. Main audit opinions, CARO, IFC, statutory exceptions and management explanations are evaluated separately.\\n\\nThe four gates are not four automatic numerical deductions. No evidenced G1 Fail or G3 Fail has been established, so the corresponding score-of-3 caps are not invoked. The unresolved G2/G3 status nevertheless prevents an investment-ready classification. Reported zero encumbrance in September is a dated positive, not a substitute for bank-liquidity evidence. Sources: [FY26 report](https://www.rappidvalves.in/assets/img/uploads/annual_report/3c0d8-annual-report-2025-26.pdf), [September shareholding filing](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/xbrl/SHP_1736091_12102026034610_WEB.xml).\\n\\n\"","\"Scorecard and calculation audit\"","\"| Composite | Raw | Final display | Coverage | Raw bounds | Effective bounds after cap |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| BQS | 4.50 | 4.5 | 100.00% | 4.500–4.500 | 4.500–4.500 |\\n| GQS | 5.85 | 5.0 | 100.00% | 5.850–5.850 | 5.000–5.000 |\\n| IAS | NS | NS | 55.00% | 2.375–6.425 | 2.375–6.425 |\\n\\n| Factor | Score | Confidence | Evidence and contrary fact |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| GOV | 4 | Medium | Main opinions are unmodified, but India backup exceptions recur in FY24–26 and material filing/note errors persist. IPO acquisition/capex money was redirected to working capital; old statutory dues and the Vinpra relationship warrant scrutiny. Contrary: No auditor-identified fraud or bank default; object variation received shareholder approval. September 2026 filing reports no pledge, NDU or other encumbrance, a dated positive not a blanket assurance. |\\n| MOAT | 5 | Medium | Customer qualification, marine engineering and product-specific factory approvals create useful switching friction. Competitors have comparable industrial products and certifications; metal-price inflation was not fully passed through. Contrary: FY24 top-five customers represented 71.88% of sales. Purchase orders do not establish permanent contracts or market share. |\\n| REV | 8 | Medium | FY21–26 nominal constant-entity operating sales grew from 7.6830956 to 53.233 crore, with no completed acquisition identified. Manufacturing gross value added rose from 9.88681 to 14.06742 to 15.492 crore in FY24–26. Contrary: The pandemic depressed FY21. FY26 growth was 2.13%, with opposing half-year trends. This is nominal constant-scope growth, not verified volume growth: starting score 9, medium-confidence ceiling 8, no trajectory adjustment. |\\n| DUR | 6 | Medium | September firm backlog was 54.36 crore; BHEL orders span five ships through FY28. Machining/testing purchases and US product qualification can broaden access. Contrary: Annual backlog rollforwards, order margins, advances and cancellations are not disclosed. US final approval remains pending. |\\n| CAP | 4 | Medium-low | Operating capital increased much faster than recent NOPAT; the one-year incremental return proxy fell to about 2.10%. The calculation includes machinery and working capital, plus a separately disclosed lagged FY24–25 deployment proxy. Contrary: Project-level commissioning cohorts are not disclosed. The lagged proxy is stronger than the latest increment; score 4 reflects mixed observed returns, not proven destruction of every project. |\\n| CASH | 1 | High | FY24–26 cumulative CFO was negative 26.12514 crore against PAT of 16.64127 crore. FY26 inventory rose 10.50486 crore and receivables 5.59962 crore despite only 1.1084 crore of sales growth. Contrary: Profits and orders are positive, and future collections could recover; neither reverses the observed cash conversion. |\\n| BAL | 3 | Medium | Debt was 17.843 crore against 0.387 crore cash/current-account proxy; deposit availability is unresolved. Gross-payment timing and principal runoff expose funding gaps; the modeled equity rescue is optional and uncommitted. Contrary: Operating interest cover and book equity provide some protection. No actual current default has been established. |\\n| EXEC | 4 | Medium | The February 2025 volume-growth target and June 2025 capex timetable were not demonstrated by FY26 results. The June 2026 equipment timetable remained procurement/advance language in July; the IPO acquisition was not executed. Contrary: Export sales and formal marine orders grew. Management acknowledged the metal-price shock and missed growth. |\\n| EARN | 5 | Medium-low | PAT grew from 6.03686 to 6.478 crore; year-end-share EPS rose from about 11.63 to 12.48 without FY26 issuance. A useful-life estimate change, the FY25 weighted-share presentation and comparative errors limit earnings precision. Contrary: Operating profit supplies most earnings. Removing other income and adding a separate D&A stress still leaves positive profits; cash conversion is not double-penalized here. |\\n| R | NS | Low | The primary base gross-cash path breaches before entry, so an ordinary investment IRR is not valid. Optional post-entry equity cannot repair a past breach or become committed finance. Contrary: Some conditional deposit-access/slow-growth paths can be valued, but they do not establish a supported current base return. |\\n| D | 2 | Medium | Linked margin, collection, inventory and debt-maturity stress overwhelms cash and the bounded rescue. Receivables and inventory are not a liquid equity floor against overdraft funding. Contrary: Product assets, profits and firm orders may retain value. There is no finding of actual insolvency or default. |\\n| P | NS | Low | Every candidate in the bounded cash-linked reverse search is retained, including failed paths. No target is bracketed between adjacent cash-feasible samples in the stated 0–60% growth grids. Contrary: This is not proof that every nonmonotonic root is absent. Improved economics still rely on unverified existing-deposit availability; P remains NS. |\\n| L | 4 | Medium | The lower 60-session mean/median is median turnover 1,955,640 rupees; the five-lakh reference requires 5.1134 sessions at 5% participation, starting band 5. A 600-share lot costs 216,600 rupees, more than twice the 97,782-rupee median daily participation allowance: one-point implementation deduction. Contrary: 59 of 60 sessions traded. Spread and current surveillance clearance are unresolved; 2% and 10% participation sensitivities are provided. |\\n\\nExact Decimal calculations are retained, not reconstructed from displayed rounded scores. BQS = .20×GOV + .20×MOAT + .12×REV + .10×DUR + .12×CAP + .10×CASH + .08×BAL + .08×EXEC = **4.50**. GQS = .40×REV + .20×DUR + .15×EARN + .15×CAP + .10×CASH = **5.85**, capped at **5.00** because persistently weak cash conversion has CASH ≤3. The cap is not an additional factor penalty.\\n\\nIAS = .25×BQS_final + .35×R + .20×D + .10×P + .10×L. Its known weighted sum is **1.925**, known coverage **55%**, and missing weight **45%**. With each missing factor bounded 1–10, IAS is **NS**, bounded **2.375–6.425**. It is not 1.925, not a renormalized known-factor average, and not a midpoint estimate. The displayed composites use one decimal; all formulas use unrounded values.\\n\\n| Hypothetical judgment | BQS raw → final | GQS raw → final | IAS effective bounds (still NS) |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| GOV −1 | 4.30 → 3.00 | 5.85 → 5.00 | 2.000–3.000 |\\n| GOV +1 | 4.70 → 4.70 | 5.85 → 5.00 | 2.425–6.475 |\\n| CAP −1 | 4.38 → 4.38 | 5.70 → 5.00 | 2.345–6.395 |\\n| CAP +1 | 4.62 → 4.62 | 6.00 → 5.00 | 2.405–6.455 |\\n\\nThese are judgment sensitivities, not changes to the actual findings. Hypothetical GOV = 3 triggers the BQS/IAS ceiling of 3, while IAS remains NS and only its effective bounds are capped. Actual GOV remains 4; CAP changes also propagate through nested BQS into IAS. Scores judge different dimensions: CASH records observed conversion; BAL records funding structure; R requires a feasible prospective return; EARN does not repeat the CFO deduction.\\n\\n\"","\"Business overview\"","\"Rappid manufactures engineered industrial and marine valves, including ball, gate, globe, butterfly, check and specialized marine applications. Its economic role is machining, assembly, testing, qualification and delivery of specified flow-control equipment, with bought-in materials and components. It is not a royalty or asset-light software model. Different diameters, metallurgy, pressure ratings and customer tests make rupee capacity dependent on product mix; management's approximately ₹120 crore capacity statement is not a standardized physical-unit denominator.\\n\\nCustomer qualification and past delivery matter, particularly where naval/shipyard specifications and approval cycles are involved. However, order execution also requires materials, work in progress, finished goods awaiting approval/dispatch and lengthy receivables. The audited record describes one valve-manufacturing segment, not separately disclosed economics for marine, industrial, export or US opportunities. Segment-level margins and incremental capital are ND. No completed acquisition was identified in the measured growth period; proposed acquisition capital was later redirected.\\n\\nThe prospectus records top-five customer concentration of 71.88% in FY24. Export growth provides diversification, but a new export geography does not by itself remove customer or project concentration. Customer advances and payable credit have not offset the observed cash investment. The company's size and customization can enable responsiveness, but also expose it to single-order timing and metal-price shocks. Sources: SIPO, S26, SCALL, SCL26.\\n\\n\"","\"Moat and industry position\"","\"The moat is moderate, not proven dominant. Qualification, engineering familiarity and reliable testing create friction against switching an approved valve mid-project. They do not demonstrate permanent pricing power, exclusivity or a defensible market share. Management described roughly ₹10–12 crore of goods withheld from dispatch while private-customer prices were renegotiated following metal inflation; PSU contracts offered less flexibility. That is evidence against treating gross margins as contractually protected.\\n\\nThree economic comparators bound the interpretation, without importing their valuation multiples:\\n- **Kirloskar Brothers** is a much broader, larger pump-and-valve franchise with diverse products, channels and customer sectors. Its scale and service infrastructure show what a stronger commercial moat can look like, but make its consolidated/standalone earnings multiple a poor direct anchor for Rappid. The FY26 virtual report is a qualitative operating benchmark, not a like-for-like accounting peer.\\n- **Hawa Engineers** is a relevant industrial-valve competitor with broad product specifications. Its FY25 report records revenue ₹121.0618 crore and PAT ₹1.5958 crore. That lower-margin historical comparator shows that a valve label does not guarantee high returns. This is FY25, not a fabricated current FY26 comparison.\\n- **Atam Valves** is nearer scale. Its May 2026 audited FY26 results record revenue ₹47.2975 crore versus ₹60.5001 crore in FY25 and PAT ₹2.4154 crore versus ₹6.3205 crore. The weak growth/margin comparison cautions against assuming sector demand always translates into profitable growth. Its Ind AS basis differs from Rappid's presentation; no raw multiple transplant is used.\\n\\nRappid's higher reported profitability is a positive relative to these weaker margin examples, but superior profitability without cash conversion is not demonstrated superior economics. Peer scope is operating comparison only, not a full peer governance audit or recommendation. Sources: [Kirloskar Brothers FY26](https://www.kirloskarpumps.com/virtual-annual-report-2026/), [Hawa FY25](https://hawaengltd.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/09/FINAL_Hawa_Engineers_Ltd___AR___2024-25.pdf), [Atam FY26 results](https://www.atamvalves.in/wp-content/uploads/Atam_Valves_Limited_FinancialResults_31032026.pdf), SIPO and SCALL.\\n\\n\"","\"Revenue growth and scale\"","\"| FY | Sales | Operating EBITDA | PAT | CFO after finance | All capex | CFO less capex | Debt | Book equity |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 2021 | 7.68 | 1.47 | 0.21 | NS | NS | NS | 9.60 | -0.46 |\\n| 2022 | 12.14 | 1.95 | 0.29 | -0.32 | 0.05 | -0.38 | 9.78 | -0.25 |\\n| 2023 | 16.40 | 2.31 | 0.58 | -1.97 | 1.01 | -2.98 | 9.93 | 3.28 |\\n| 2024 | 36.51 | 7.35 | 4.13 | -2.48 | 2.72 | -5.20 | 10.98 | 12.37 |\\n| 2025 | 52.12 | 9.93 | 6.04 | -12.82 | 3.38 | -16.19 | 8.41 | 45.25 |\\n| 2026 | 53.23 | 10.31 | 6.48 | -10.83 | 1.79 | -12.62 | 17.84 | 51.71 |\\n\\n| FY | Absolute sales addition | Operating EBIT | Normalized PAT | Current-share EPS comparator | ROCE | ROE | Average AR / inventory / payable days |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 2021 | NS | 1.34 | 0.18 | 0.41 | NS | NS | NS / NS / NS |\\n| 2022 | 4.46 | 1.74 | 0.21 | 0.56 | 18.64% | NS | NS / NS / NS |\\n| 2023 | 4.26 | 2.01 | 0.42 | 1.11 | 17.65% | NS | NS / NS / NS |\\n| 2024 | 20.11 | 6.84 | 4.08 | 7.95 | 37.41% | 52.75% | 57.9 / 127.3 / 38.9 |\\n| 2025 | 15.61 | 9.15 | 6.02 | 11.63 | 23.76% | 20.96% | 96.5 / 133.1 / 35.5 |\\n| 2026 | 1.11 | 9.67 | 6.26 | 12.48 | 15.70% | 13.36% | 151.1 / 207.2 / 27.8 |\\n\\nROCE here is operating EBIT / average opening-and-closing (book equity + interest-bearing debt), including preference equity once. ROE is all-equity-owner PAT / average total book equity (including preference capital), not ordinary-equity ROE, reported only when both equity endpoints are positive. FY22 is NM for negative equity; FY23's crossing from negative equity is also NM. The FY24 preference transaction and FY25 IPO materially change these denominators, so high historical ROE is not organic shareholder compounding. The EPS column applies current 5,191,984 shares to each year's PAT attributable to ordinary shares where separately disclosed (FY24), otherwise PAT, solely as a constant-share scale comparator; it is not historical reported or weighted EPS. Average working-capital days use average opening/closing balances and current-year sales, adjusted material cost and purchases respectively. Closing and average proxies are both retained rather than presented as exact customer settlement terms.\\n\\nAll amounts above are crore. FY22 CFO is restated IPO CFO of ₹1.1376 crore **before** finance less finance cost ₹1.4616 crore, yielding negative ₹0.3240 crore on the later after-finance convention. FY23–26 CFO already includes finance cost, because the cash-flow reconciliation starts at PBT without adding it back and financing cash flow does not deduct it separately. Interest is therefore **not subtracted twice**. FY21 comparable CFO/capex is unavailable in the selected reconciled history and remains NS.\\n\\nFY21–26 nominal operating-sales CAGR is **47.27%**; FY23–26 is **48.07%**. The depressed FY21 pandemic base matters: FY20 revenue was ₹9.4209241 crore against FY21 ₹7.6830956 crore. FY19 comparative revenue was ₹4.5073996 crore. Growth was not smooth. FY26 increased just 2.13%; rounded H1 sales rose from ₹19.62 to ₹28.82 crore, while H2 fell from ₹32.51 to ₹24.41 crore. July's subsequent 28% sales-growth update is encouraging but unaudited and not a substitute for cash and margin data.\\n\\nRevenue is classified as **nominal, constant-entity organic operating-sales growth**, with medium confidence, not verified unit/volume growth. No acquired perimeter has been identified, but price, volume and mix contributions cannot be separated. Gross manufacturing value added, revenue less material/stock-adjusted cost, rose ₹9.88681 → ₹14.06742 → ₹15.492 crore across FY24–26. This provides an additional operating-scale check rather than giving full credit to gross material pass-through. It is not cash value added.\\n\\nFY26 exports increased ₹4.392 → ₹10.203 crore, while domestic sales declined ₹47.066 → ₹42.611 crore. The net unbilled-revenue movement declined ₹0.667 → ₹0.419 crore. These changes explain the approximately ₹1.1084 crore total increase, subject to statement rounding. The incorrect gross unbilled figures in Note 21 are not used to inflate sales or claim a giant contract asset. Sources: S20–S26, SIPO, SDECK, SJUL.\\n\\n\"","\"Growth quality and reinvestment runway\"","\"| FY | Operating WC | Operating IC | NOPAT | Average-IC ROIC | ΔIC | ΔNOPAT / ΔIC | AR / inventory / payable days |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 2023 | 7.83 | 12.34 | 1.50 | NS | NS | NS | 72.8 / 215.4 / 71.0 |\\n| 2024 | 15.96 | 22.64 | 5.13 | 29.32% | 10.29 | 35.21% | 83.2 / 158.8 / 46.8 |\\n| 2025 | 35.86 | 45.11 | 6.86 | 20.25% | 22.48 | 7.71% | 134.7 / 155.1 / 37.4 |\\n| 2026 | 53.58 | 63.91 | 7.26 | 13.31% | 18.80 | 2.10% | 170.3 / 258.0 / 22.3 |\\n\\nOperating IC = net PPE + inventory + trade receivables + short-term operating advances + other current assets excluding identified tax assets − payables − other operating liabilities − operating provisions + operating security deposits. It excludes cash, bank deposits and deferred-tax assets; preference equity already within book equity is not counted again as debt. NOPAT = (PBT − other income + finance cost) ×75%, using an explicit analytical 25% tax assumption. Average-IC ROIC uses opening and closing IC; days are closing-balance proxies, not exact invoice-aging statistics. Inventory days use adjusted material cost; payable days use purchases, so the cash-cycle proxy is an approximation.\\n\\n| FY | Δ operating WC | Δ PPE | Δ security | Cash capex − P&L D&A | PPE reconciliation residual |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 2024 | 8.12523 | 2.17268 | -0.00510 | 2.20791 | -0.03523 |\\n| 2025 | 19.90308 | 2.55955 | 0.01524 | 2.59304 | -0.03349 |\\n| 2026 | 17.72473 | 1.04169 | 0.03122 | 1.15900 | -0.11731 |\\n\\n| FY | Equity + debt − cash − nonoperating investments | Tax liabilities − tax assets − DTA | Residual | Operating IC |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 2023 | 12.39741 | -0.05475 | 0.00002 | 12.34268 |\\n| 2024 | 21.59819 | 1.03729 | 0.00001 | 22.63549 |\\n| 2025 | 43.60026 | 1.51310 | 0.00000 | 45.11336 |\\n| 2026 | 62.29400 | 1.61900 | -0.00200 | 63.91100 |\\n\\nThe financing bridge subtracts reported cash (including its short deposits) and separately identified noncurrent deposits/unquoted investments, irrespective of spendability. Income-tax liabilities are added back and current tax assets/deferred-tax assets removed to match operating IC. Small remaining residuals are retained for rounding/classification, not plugged into returns. FY26's restricted-deposit treatment in valuation is a separate availability question, not an accounting reclassification.\\n\\nThe capital bridge is additive: ΔIC = Δ operating WC + Δ PPE + Δ operating security deposits. Cash capex minus P&L D&A need not equal Δ PPE: disposals, revaluation-reserve depreciation, classification and cash/noncash timing remain in the explicit residual above. FY26's asset schedule has about ₹0.6462 crore depreciation, including about ₹0.0111 crore charged to revaluation reserve, versus P&L D&A ₹0.635 crore; cash disposal proceeds are not assumed equal to disposed net book value. No residual is silently set to zero.\\n\\nThree-year incremental NOPAT/ΔIC is **11.15%**. A lagged crosscheck uses FY24–25 company-wide IC deployment against FY26 less FY23 NOPAT and gives **17.55%**. That cohort has one to two subsequent operating years, but neither machine-by-machine commissioning dates nor attributable project cash flows are available. It is a lag sensitivity, not a certified project IRR. The FY24–25 investment in machinery and working capital is therefore not dismissed as wholly unseasoned, while the more recent July 2026 equipment purchases are not assumed already commissioned. Precise five-year incremental ROIC is **NM** because early loans, advances, provisions, revaluation and restated balance-sheet classifications cannot be bridged reliably enough. Revenue CAGR does not require that missing IC precision.\\n\\nThe near-term runway is tangible but capital hungry. The ₹18.0576 crore BHEL five-ship order (excluding taxes) places three shipsets around a year and two into FY27–28. The ₹8.55 crore Shree order converted a prior LOI into a formal order; it is not counted twice. July disclosed ₹40 crore firm orders plus ₹11 crore customer confirmations awaiting POs; the latter are not firm backlog. The three large Q1 orders totaling approximately ₹29.85 crore are inside that book, not additive. August's ₹2.84 crore L&T order requires delivery ten weeks **after drawing/QAP approval**, not ten weeks from the announcement. September's ₹54.36 crore backlog is the later snapshot, including ₹0.8531 crore HSL and ₹1.56 crore Solas orders due by March 2027.\\n\\nThe September US update is product/factory-specific final qualification testing, not completed UL approval, exclusivity or commercial purchase orders. The undisclosed prospective customer cannot be modeled as assured revenue. The February **2025** announcement targeting 50% volume CAGR in FY26/FY27 and capex completion in June 2025 must be dated correctly. FY26 delivery fell short; June 2026 procurement of ₹1.25 crore equipment was expected within 25 days, while July still described POs/advances. Neither a ₹120 crore mix-dependent capacity claim nor six test benches establishes audited throughput or utilization. Sources: SCALL, SPROMISE, SJUL, SBHEL, SSHREE, SAUG, SSEP, SUS.\\n\\n\"","\"Management, governance and execution\"","\"The September 30 shareholding filing, submitted October 12, reports promoter-group shares 2,685,736 of 5,191,984, reported 51.73%; public shareholding is reported 48.27%. It explicitly reports no pledge, non-disposal undertaking or other encumbrance. Promoter lock-in is 1,100,000 shares, or reported **40.96% of promoter shares**, not 40.96% of the entire company. Lock-in is not pledge. The April FY26 declaration of no newly created encumbrance is a different period/test and is not used as a substitute for September's direct filing. [Primary September filing](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/xbrl/SHP_1736091_12102026034610_WEB.xml).\\n\\nIPO gross proceeds were ₹30.40956 crore, from 1,369,800 shares at ₹222. The original objects included debt repayment, capex/renovation, acquisition and general corporate purposes. The FY26 report shows capex ₹3.086 crore utilized against the ₹6.731 crore object, leaving approximately ₹3.6451 crore; ₹4 crore acquisition money remained unused. The ₹7.6451 crore variation to working capital was approved through the April 2026 shareholder process. The contemporaneous voting record points to April 29 results disclosed April 30, not an assumed April 17 approval merely because Note 32 says EGM April 17. Existing deposits are smaller than the unutilized-object total and are not automatically legally or operationally available cash.\\n\\nThis is capital-allocation drift: an acquisition/capacity thesis became working-capital funding. Approval mitigates unauthorized-use concerns but does not prove attractive returns. The pre-IPO ₹4.98 crore convertible-preference transaction and September 2024 IPO must not be redated to 2025 based on erroneous Note 3 narrative. Verified ordinary shares remain 5,191,984 at the October market date. The model prohibits pre-entry equity issuance rather than rewriting that observed share count.\\n\\nRelated-party scrutiny is material. The IPO restatement shows FY24 sales to Vinpra of ₹7.657 crore, approximately 20.97% of FY24 revenue, versus much smaller earlier amounts. A director's prior resignation does not alone establish the later absence of influence; omission from the latest RPT list does not settle current customer/relationship status. FY24 Vinpra closing receivables were ₹1.5893 crore; the schedule's credits are not automatically bank receipts. Obtain current beneficial relationship, balances, collection and arm's-length terms. FY22–26 disclosed remuneration/professional fees/rent are retained in the evidence file, not only the latest year. FY26 disclosed Gaurav remuneration ₹0.62 crore, Vijay ₹0.12 crore, Mansi ₹0.28 crore and rent ₹0.026 crore, plus independent-director amounts. Evaluate these against profits and liquidity, not as automatic abuse.\\n\\nExecution positives include export expansion and formal marine orders; negatives include missed growth/capex timing, the unused acquisition allocation and repeated filing corrections. June 2025 correspondence acknowledged a missing unmodified-opinion declaration/format questions; January 2026 clarified the single operating segment. Those are disclosure weaknesses, not evidence that the company admitted a profit restatement or fraud. Sources: SIPO, S26, SOBJECT, SCL25, SCL26, SSHP26, audit_evidence.json.\\n\\n\"","\"Financial forensics\"","\"### Audit chronology, including annexures\\n- **FY19 bundle:** its appended signed main/IFC report is explicitly for FY18, dated September 5, 2018, by Amrut Deshmukh. It cannot be counted as FY19 audit assurance.\\n- **FY20:** signed Parab & Kadam opinion is unmodified, October 5, 2020, although directors' text names another firm. CARO says inventory procedures were reasonable but **not adequate** and records historical PF/ESIC/professional-tax arrears beyond six months. No bank default is reported.\\n- **FY21:** Kalantri Assawa's unmodified opinion is dated October 30, 2021. The main report calls CARO inapplicable, yet an annexure contains irregular statutory deposits/old arrears. A remuneration row is incomplete boilerplate. No fraud/bank default is reported.\\n- **FY22:** Vijay Bhardwaj's September 26, 2022 opinion is unmodified; CARO is stated inapplicable and no separate positive IFC conclusion is inferred.\\n- **FY23:** Vijay Bhardwaj's September 6, 2023 opinion is unmodified; CARO is stated inapplicable. The incomplete IFC sentence is not affirmative assurance.\\n- **FY24:** KAVA's June 12, 2024 main opinion is unmodified, but the India daily-backup exception appears in the books/legal reporting. TDS ₹14,37,671 and PF ₹19,454 were overdue beyond six months. IFC exemption language is not a positive IFC audit opinion.\\n- **FY25:** KAVA's main opinion is unmodified; the India-backup exception repeats. Internal-audit and private-company remuneration language conflict with listed-company/corporate disclosures. Read the appended IFC conclusion separately.\\n- **FY26:** KAVA/Vivek Jalan's May 27, 2026 main opinion is unmodified, with adequate IFC opinion and a separate positive audit-trail statement. The daily India-backup exception nevertheless remains. CARO says there is no internal-audit system while corporate information names an internal auditor. It reports no fraud, bank default or auditor resignation, and no identified one-year material uncertainty. These are not a guarantee of future liquidity. Later no-over-six-month-dues wording does not establish payment dates of older arrears.\\n\\n### Accounting and cash reconciliation\\nReported FY26 EPS of 0.1 on the P&L face is a scale error; PAT divided by verified shares gives about ₹12.48, consistent with Note 30's approximate ₹12.5. FY25's 5,192 thousand denominator appears to use year-end shares despite midyear IPO issuance, so a true weighted-average comparative EPS is not reconstructed without a share-date calculation. The FY25 common-share comparator of about ₹11.63 is expressly a **year-end-share** measure, not a claim that the printed weighted EPS is reliable.\\n\\nFY26 Note 2.6 prospectively revises useful-life/residual estimates; the benefit is not quantified. H2 D&A of roughly ₹0.12 crore versus H1 ₹0.52 crore heightens normalization uncertainty. Operating-only normalized PAT at a 25% analytical tax rate is ₹6.25875 crore. An additional ₹0.15 crore pretax D&A sensitivity reduces it to ₹6.14625 crore. This is a stress, not an invented official estimate adjustment.\\n\\nThe Note 21 gross unbilled opening/closing figures (₹5,267.8/₹5,309.7 lakh) conflict with Note 18's ₹119.4/₹161.3 lakh. The net increase ₹41.9 lakh agrees. Use the balance-sheet amounts; retain the gross-note error. Generic no-contingency wording does not erase the explicitly disclosed ₹0.146 crore bank guarantee. IPO tax proceedings and later generic nonmaterial-dispute language do not prove settlement; current closure documents are ND. The printed DSCR has denominator/unit problems and is not adopted; independently computed operating EBIT/finance is used instead.\\n\\nFY26 cash flow reconciles PBT ₹8.664 + D&A ₹0.635 − long-term provisions ₹0.030 − the presentation's short-term-provision/tax adjustment ₹2.108 − other income ₹0.319 − inventory ₹10.505 − receivables ₹5.599 − advances ₹0.010 − other assets ₹0.162 − payables ₹1.352 + other liabilities ₹0.460 − tax paid ₹0.500 = **−₹10.826 crore**. The short-term provision line embeds current-tax adjustment; advance tax and tax-paid presentation are not deducted twice. CFO −₹10.826, investing −₹5.589 and financing +₹9.428 reconcile cash from ₹9.494 to ₹2.507 crore. That closing cash number includes ₹2.120 crore sub-12-month deposits; cash/current accounts are only ₹0.387 crore. Another ₹4.756 crore deposits are longer term.\\n\\nThree-year CFO/PAT is approximately **−157%**. FY26 inventory increased ₹10.50486 crore and AR ₹5.59962 crore; payable reduction also consumed funding. This is not explained away by PAT growth or listing proceeds. Deposits, IPO object balances, tax liabilities and overdrafts must be reconciled through bank evidence before using an accounting net-cash figure as spendable liquidity.\\n\\n### Historical vintages retained, not spliced invisibly\\nIPO-restated FY22 PAT ₹0.2898 differs from original ₹0.3243699. FY23 original PAT ₹0.5759394, later comparative ₹0.57578 and IPO-restated ₹0.4556 are distinct vintages; original AR/payable/other-asset classifications also differ materially. The selected later-comparative basis is identified in historical_inputs.json, alongside every alternative.\\n\\nFY24 IPO-restated CFO −₹1.0733 crore is before finance ₹1.4029; after finance it is −₹2.4762, versus later-comparative −₹2.48153, residual −₹0.00533 crore. This reconciles most of the apparent CFO discrepancy but does **not** resolve all cash/equity restatement differences: IPO cash ₹1.5830 includes deposits versus later cash ₹1.22293; equity ₹12.2198 versus ₹12.36796. The original FY24 CFO rounding difference of ₹0.00007 crore is separately immaterial. Restatement auditor Vinay Bhushan relied on prior auditors; it does not create identical accounting vintages. All audit observations, RPT history and reconciliation limitations are retained in the standalone inputs. Sources: S19–S26, SN26, SIPO.\\n\\n\"","\"Valuation and market expectations\"","\"Current reported P/E is **28.93×**, operating-only normalized P/E **29.95×**, and D&A-stress P/E **30.50×**. EV/operating EBITDA is **19.87×**, using only ₹0.387 crore freely available cash proxy against debt, not assuming all deposits free. EV is October market capitalization combined with the March 31 audited balance-sheet anchor, not a verified October net-debt figure. These are descriptive multiples, not supported fair-value targets. Deposit accessibility can lower accounting net debt but cannot repair cash timing after a prior breach.\\n\\n### Linked cash model and financing bounds\\nThe model starts March 31 with ₹0.387 crore cash, ₹6.876 crore deposits, ₹17.843 crore debt, ₹24.832 crore AR, ₹26.675 crore inventory, ₹2.767 crore payables, ₹4.843 crore other net operating WC and ₹10.278 crore PPE. The 203-day pre-entry bridge includes a distinct April 29 interval **before** an optional April 30 deposit release. A separate, clearly unverified alternative assumes lawful availability of existing deposits at the March anchor. Neither alternative creates new IPO cash.\\n\\nPre-entry growth rates are 0/12/25% bear/base/bull, a ₹1.25 crore equipment cash allocation, ₹1.901 crore old tax settlement in June, a ₹0.25 crore cash floor, 11% debt interest and 4% deposit income. Tax timing and monthly growth are assumptions, not bank observations. Growth capex is spread across the bridge rather than falsely asserting an exact historical payment date. The forward base begins from an annualized bridge run-rate of about ₹59.62 crore, then grows to about ₹66.78 crore in its first full forward year; this is a transparent run-rate convention, not a reported FY27 forecast.\\n\\nEach period separately models customer receipts = revenue − ΔAR; supplier payments = purchased-material cost + Δinventory − Δpayables; payroll/other opex; other WC; current cash taxes; maintenance/growth capex; interest; old taxes; loss replenishment; principal; and remaining customer receipts. Fifty percent of receipts precede payments in the central timing assumption. Gross outflow-first, 25% and 100% front-loaded receipts are separate sensitivities. This is a bounded monthly settlement-cycle stress, not a claim to know daily invoice dates; additional within-month clustering remains a risk.\\n\\nBase forward assumptions: 12% annual sales growth, 18% EBITDA margin, AR 170 days, inventory 240 purchased-material days, payable 25 days, other net WC 9% of sales and PPE requirement 20% of sales. Bear uses flat sales, 14% margin, AR190/inventory280/payable20, plus 10% opening AR/inventory loss and guarantee call. Bull uses 25% growth, 21% margin and improved AR140/inventory190/payable35. The denominator for modeled inventory is material purchases, not the historical adjusted-COGS day ratio. All assumptions and alternatives are explicit, not optimized to force a target.\\n\\nMaintenance capex is 1.2× modeled D&A, with 1.0× and 1.5× sensitivities; D&A is 7% of rolling PPE. Growth capex is the incremental required fixed-asset base, while maintenance above D&A also accumulates PPE. Debt principal is repaid in month 60 in base/bull and month 12 in bear; a month-1 no-renewal stress is included. No new bank line or perpetual refinancing is assumed.\\n\\nOptional equity is allowed **only after entry**, only if the bridge has not failed, at ₹288.80 (20% below entry), 2% issuance costs, gross cap ₹15 crore, maximum 1,000,000 new shares and authorized ceiling 10,172,000 shares. Required shares are rounded **up** to a whole share, capacity rounded down. The ₹15 crore limit binds before the one-million-share ceiling. Just-in-time completion is deliberately optimistic and **uncommitted**. A failed bridge cannot be rescued retrospectively. No ordinary IRR is emitted after a cash-floor breach, even if later cash becomes positive.\\n\\n| Path | First breach (scenario event) | Minimum cash | Equity gross raised | Final shares | Ordinary / conditional IRR |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| bear_none | 2026-04-29 supplier_settlement_including_inventory_and_payables | -31.37 | 0.00 | 5191984 | NS / NS |\\n| base_none | 2026-04-29 supplier_settlement_including_inventory_and_payables | -33.77 | 0.00 | 5191984 | NS / NS |\\n| bull_none | 2026-04-29 supplier_settlement_including_inventory_and_payables | -51.76 | 0.00 | 5191984 | NS / NS |\\n| bear_bounded_equity_conditional_anchor_access | 2027-10-20 principal_repayment_no_automatic_rollover | -10.10 | 15.00 | 5711374 | NS / NS |\\n| base_bounded_equity_conditional_anchor_access | 2031-10-20 principal_repayment_no_automatic_rollover | -13.32 | 15.00 | 5711374 | NS / NS |\\n| bull_bounded_equity_conditional_anchor_access | 2026-08-31 current_period_cash_tax | -46.01 | 0.00 | 5191984 | NS / NS |\\n\\nBase no-financing annual trajectory below is an **infeasible diagnostic**, not an investable forecast. Continuing its arithmetic retains the failure and quantifies funding demand; negative cash is never presented as a valid financing balance.\\n\\n| Year to October | Revenue | EBITDA | Interest | Tax | ΔWC | Maintenance | Growth capex | Principal | Closing cash |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 2027 | 66.78 | 12.02 | 1.96 | 2.30 | 8.27 | 1.05 | 1.74 | 0.00 | -6.06 |\\n| 2028 | 74.79 | 13.46 | 1.96 | 2.63 | 7.85 | 1.20 | 1.43 | 0.00 | -7.46 |\\n| 2029 | 83.76 | 15.08 | 1.96 | 3.00 | 8.79 | 1.34 | 1.60 | 0.00 | -8.87 |\\n| 2030 | 93.81 | 16.89 | 1.96 | 3.42 | 9.85 | 1.50 | 1.79 | 0.00 | -10.30 |\\n| 2031 | 105.07 | 18.91 | 1.96 | 3.89 | 11.03 | 1.68 | 2.00 | 17.84 | -29.59 |\\n\\n### Earned terminal valuation, not arbitrary exit P/E\\nFor every path, a further 12-month gross-cash continuation is calculated with no new financing. Forward NOPAT + D&A − maintenance − growth capex − incremental WC yields earned FCFF. EV = FCFF/(discount rate − terminal growth), only for positive FCFF. The central rate/growth is 14%/3%, with 12/14/16% ×2/3/4% grid. An explicit operating reserve covers the most negative cumulative intra-period cash draw plus the floor; only cash **above that reserve** is excess cash. Terminal equity = EV + excess cash − remaining debt, divided by fully diluted shares. Restricted deposits are excluded, with a separately marked recoverability sensitivity. A terminal cash breach, negative recurring continuation cash or nonpositive FCFF disqualifies returns.\\n\\nForward operating/incremental returns, reinvestment, terminal reserve, all cash events and shares are retained for every candidate. Entry DCF is an unlevered diagnostic before future dilution and never treats later issue proceeds as undiluted value accruing to existing shareholders. No terminal multiple is awarded merely because a company has survived five years.\\n\\nBest among all retained feasible sensitivity paths (not a supported base): p_489428e1605013eaed33, terminal price ₹417.58, conditional wealth 1.1567×, conditional annual return 2.95%.\\n\\nThis conditional path assumes 25% forward growth, 22% EBITDA margin, 120-day AR, 150-day purchased-material inventory, 40-day payables, other net WC of 7% of sales, PPE intensity of 18%, full unverified anchor-deposit access, and optional bounded equity. Its entry shares remain 5191984, final shares 5434052, equity gross raised ₹6.99 crore, and post-entry dilution 4.45%. The 12% pre-entry bridge-growth assumption remains distinct from forward growth.\\n\\n| Discount rate | Terminal growth | Conditional terminal price | Conditional IRR |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 12.00% | 2.00% | 480.35 | 5.88% |\\n| 12.00% | 3.00% | 509.80 | 7.15% |\\n| 12.00% | 4.00% | 546.68 | 8.65% |\\n| 14.00% | 2.00% | 400.77 | 2.11% |\\n| 14.00% | 3.00% | 417.58 | 2.95% |\\n| 14.00% | 4.00% | 437.81 | 3.93% |\\n| 16.00% | 2.00% | 343.93 | -0.96% |\\n| 16.00% | 3.00% | 353.74 | -0.41% |\\n| 16.00% | 4.00% | 365.23 | 0.23% |\\n\\nThe following ceilings discount that **best sampled conditional** price, not a supported base price. They are sensitivity outputs, **not buy levels**, because the deposit bridge, implementation and earnings-quality gates remain unresolved. A lower market quote cannot itself solve an issuer funding gap.\\n\\n| Required 5Y annual return | Entry ceiling from best sampled conditional price | Wealth hurdle |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| 12.00% | 236.95 | 1.76234 |\\n| 15.00% | 207.61 | 2.01136 |\\n| 18.00% | 182.53 | 2.28776 |\\n\\n### Reverse expectations and two-variable sensitivity\\n| Economics | Financing | Feasible sampled growth | Maximum sampled wealth | Search result |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| unchanged_economics | none | 0% | 0.3336 | No adjacent feasible bracket at 12/15/18% IRR, 5× or 10× |\\n| unchanged_economics | bounded_equity | 0%, 5% | 0.3382 | No adjacent feasible bracket at 12/15/18% IRR, 5× or 10× |\\n| cash_turnaround_counterfactual | none | 0%, 5%, 10%, 15%, 20% | 1.0326 | No adjacent feasible bracket at 12/15/18% IRR, 5× or 10× |\\n| cash_turnaround_counterfactual | bounded_equity | 0%, 5%, 10%, 15%, 20%, 25% | 1.1567 | No adjacent feasible bracket at 12/15/18% IRR, 5× or 10× |\\n\\nEach of four regime/funding combinations tests 0–60% forward sales growth in 5-point steps, holding the bridge separately at the base pre-growth assumption. Targets are 12%,15%,18% annual return, 5× and 10× wealth. Only adjacent cash-feasible samples can bracket bisection; every failed grid/root candidate retains its complete cash path. No extrapolation crosses a funding failure. The result is **no feasible adjacent bracket in these bounded sampled grids**, not mathematical proof that every nonmonotonic root is absent. The 22% margin/AR120/inventory150/payable40 turnaround is explicitly a counterfactual, not guidance.\\n\\nTwo-way table: terminal price / conditional IRR with 12% forward growth, optional bounded equity and full **unverified** anchor-deposit access. NS cells failed cash/continuation tests; they are not assigned fictional IRRs.\\n\\n| EBITDA margin / AR days | 120 | 150 | 170 | 200 |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 16.00% | NS: cash failure | NS: cash failure | NS: cash failure | NS: cash failure |\\n| 18.00% | 163.67 / -14.63% | NS: cash failure | NS: cash failure | NS: cash failure |\\n| 20.00% | 198.33 / -11.29% | NS: cash failure | NS: cash failure | NS: cash failure |\\n| 22.00% | 236.22 / -8.13% | 218.81 / -9.53% | 208.24 / -10.42% | NS: cash failure |\\n\\nAll 97 distinct normalized paths, their 9 terminal valuations, test aliases and reverse-attempt aliases are retained losslessly. Identical assumptions share one complete path, not duplicate megabytes; a failed path is never deleted to make the search appear successful. Sources: MODEL assumptions, S26, SCALL, SOBJECT; model_engine.py and model_inputs.json.\\n\\n\"","\"Bear case and permanent-loss channels\"","\"The central risk chain is slower customer approvals/collections plus inventory buildup, followed by metal or fixed-price margin pressure, overdraft dependence, tax/supplier payments and debt nonrenewal. The equity holder can lose through both business impairment and financing dilution. Positive EBITDA does not pay a supplier before cash arrives. A book-equity figure backed by specialized stock and slow receivables is not a liquidation floor.\\n\\nThe explicit bear links 10% opening AR impairment, 10% inventory loss/replenishment, the disclosed guarantee call, weaker margin and early principal runoff. Even conditional opening deposit access plus the nearly ₹15 crore modeled issue does not survive its principal payment. Cash failure yields **NS ordinary and conditional IRR**, not a spurious bounded negative return. Recovery value after an insolvency or restructuring is ND; no arbitrary terminal P/E is applied to a failed going concern.\\n\\nOther loss channels are customer concentration, unbilled/billing disputes, supplier terms, repeated report-control weaknesses, capex yielding less usable capacity than claimed, delayed US approval and inability to execute a 600-share lot at reasonable participation. These are risks, not accusations of fraud or statements that a future default is certain. No lending covenant headroom or committed refinancing has been verified.\\n\\n\"","\"5× / 10× potential\"","\"Fivefold wealth in five years requires about 37.97% annual return; tenfold requires about 58.49%. At ₹361 these require at least ₹1,805/₹3,610 per fully diluted share at the horizon, absent dividends. Required equity value rises further with new shares. Multiplying today's shares by a hypothetical target while ignoring issuance would understate the hurdle.\\n\\nNeither target is bracketed by adjacent cash-feasible candidates in the bounded reverse grids. Higher growth absorbs more working capital and fixed investment, which can exhaust finance before the terminal business reaches the proposed size. Commercial capacity/order opportunity is not equivalent to funded shareholder compounding. The thesis would need demonstrated collections, lower working-capital intensity, attractive **incremental** capital returns, disciplined capex, dependable financing and sustained operating margins, not simply an optimistic exit multiple. A 5×/10× claim is therefore unsupported at this price and evidence set, rather than declared mathematically impossible for all future outcomes.\\n\\n\"","\"Monitoring checklist and falsification tests\"","\"1. Obtain March-to-current bank/deposit statements or a credible filed reconciliation: lien, maturity, IPO-object restrictions, withdrawal authority and actual deployment. Reconcile every release against cash and the shareholder approval date.\\n2. Obtain September/next-half financials and cash flow, not only sales or order announcements. Track receivable aging, subsequent receipts, inventory aging/held dispatch and customer advances. Positive CFO with reduced inventory/AR intensity is the key improvement; mere PAT growth is insufficient.\\n3. Reconcile overdraft sanctioned/utilized limits, maturity/renewal, covenant terms, security and bank guarantees. A committed new line must be evidenced before entering the committed-finance case.\\n4. Reconcile the IPO utilization statement, capex completion/commissioning, acquisition cancellation and working-capital variation. Match equipment invoices, delivery, installation and throughput rather than treating POs as completion.\\n5. Ask for quantified useful-life-change impact, corrected EPS/share chronology, unbilled note, DSCR, comparative financial vintages and an explanation of the internal-audit contradiction and India-backup remedy.\\n6. Follow Vinpra's relationship status, current sales/balances and collection terms; monitor promoter remuneration against cash generation. Maintain dated pledge/encumbrance checks without confusing lock-in with pledge.\\n7. Reconcile backlog opening + new firm orders − recognized/delivered orders − cancellations = closing. Keep LOIs, customer confirmations and US qualification separate from formal orders. Verify L&T approval dates and BHEL delivery milestones.\\n8. Before any trade, verify live bid/offer depth, active series, lot, circuit and ASM/GSM/ESM/trade-to-trade status. The static October security master is not a dated all-clear for surveillance.\\n\\nA falsified cash thesis would be sustained cash collections and profitable deliveries without recurring external equity, supported by a full capital bridge. A falsified moat thesis would be repeated repricing inability, lost approvals or margin compression despite backlog. A price decline alone falsifies neither.\\n\\n\"","\"Final classification and conditional action\"","\"**Hold — unresolved / research only. BQS 4.50; GQS 5.00 after cap; IAS NS (55% coverage, bounded 2.375–6.425).** This is not an existing-position instruction and not a personalized allocation recommendation. It is an evidence-based classification as of the stated cutoffs.\\n\\nLiquidity is investability, not just turnover. The official 60-session sample (July24–October19) contains 59 traded sessions and one absent issuer row on September4 in an otherwise complete exchange archive, correctly counted as zero. Mean turnover is ₹4,035,899.50; median ₹1,955,640, the lower denominator. A ₹5 lakh reference exit requires 5.113415557 sessions at 5%, versus 12.78353889 at 2% and 2.556707779 at 10%. These are continuous arithmetic estimates, not executable promises. At volume-based 5% participation, only 13 of the historical 60 sessions permitted even one whole 600-share lot (3 at 2%, 33 at 10%). This is a historical capacity count, not a future exit schedule. A 600-share lot costs ₹216,600; two lots cost ₹433,200, three ₹649,800, so ₹5 lakh cannot be implemented exactly. One lot exceeds the ₹97,782 median daily 5% allowance. The 20-session mean/median are ₹4,076,001/₹2,254,575. There is no claim that the master's market-maker-spread field is a live spread. Its ₹288.80–₹433.20 band indicates a 20% static price band, not verified current restriction clearance.\\n\\nDo not upgrade to investment consideration until the funding bridge and data questions are resolved and a cash-feasible base case clears the desired return with realistic dilution and lot execution. Any conditional price ceiling above is subordinate to those gates. No purchase, sale or publication is authorized by this report alone.\\n\\n\"","\"Sources, assumptions and open questions\"","\"Source hierarchy: signed audited statements/notes and exchange filings first; contemporaneous management call/deck/order announcements second; economic interpretation and model assumptions separately labeled. Aggregate official market/shareholding extracts are retained with source hashes. Source bodies, copyrighted PDFs/OCR, raw shareholder personal records, private service identifiers, transfer URLs and helper code are excluded from deliverables.\\n\\n| ID | Primary document / locator |\\n| --- | --- |\\n| S26 | [FY2026 issuer annual report](https://www.rappidvalves.in/assets/img/uploads/annual_report/3c0d8-annual-report-2025-26.pdf); Printed pages 54–69 notice; 90–105 main audit, CARO and IFC; 106–139 accounts and notes. Issuer PDF uses double-page spreads. |\\n| S25 | [FY2025 issuer annual report](https://www.rappidvalves.in/assets/img/uploads/annual_report/b1782-annual-report-2024-25.pdf); Printed pages 71–79 main audit and annexures; 80–108 accounts and notes (physical pages 86–114). |\\n| S24 | [FY2024 issuer annual report](https://www.rappidvalves.in/assets/img/uploads/annual_report/88dc0-annual-report_2023-24.pdf); Physical pages 1 and 4–8 main audit/CARO; 9–19 financial statements and notes. |\\n| S23 | [FY2023 issuer annual report](https://www.rappidvalves.in/assets/img/uploads/annual_report/d92a7-annual-report-2022-23.pdf); Physical pages 10–11 signed audit; 12–22 accounts and notes. No separate positive CARO/IFC conclusion. |\\n| S22 | [FY2022 issuer annual report](https://www.rappidvalves.in/assets/img/uploads/annual_report/47b2b-annual-report-2021-22.pdf); Physical pages 10–12 signed audit; 13–23 financials, including FY21 comparative. |\\n| S21 | [FY2021 issuer annual report](https://www.rappidvalves.in/assets/img/uploads/annual_report/0ad71-annual-report_2020-21.pdf); Physical pages 16–20 audit and annexure; 21–41 accounts and notes. |\\n| S20 | [FY2020 issuer annual report](https://www.rappidvalves.in/assets/img/uploads/annual_report/0c6f8-annual-report_2019-20.pdf); Physical pages 5–10 signed audit and annexure; 11–18 financial statements. |\\n| S19 | [FY2019 issuer annual report](https://www.rappidvalves.in/assets/img/uploads/annual_report/9af51-annual-report_2018-19.pdf); Appended signed opinion concerns FY18, not FY19. Historical financial-vintage gap retained. |\\n| SN26 | [FY26 independent exchange annual report](https://archives.nseindia.com/annual_reports/SME_AR_31041_RAPPID_2025_2026_A_59306041_01092026201328.pdf); Same statutory report, independent exchange copy; audited accounts and audit annexures |\\n| SIPO | [Final September2024 prospectus](https://www.rappidvalves.in/assets/img/uploads/initial_public_offer/dd504-prospectus_rappid.pdf); Risk factors and customer concentration; capital history; IPO objects; F1–F13 restatement and accounting basis; F29 related-party schedule; management discussion. |\\n| SCALL | [June1 earnings call](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/RAPPID_03062026163806_Rappid_Transcript_Signed.pdf); entire call; cost escalation, held dispatch, guidance, equipment, capacity and liquidity |\\n| SDECK | [June1 investor presentation](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/RAPPID_01062026141424_Exchange_PresentationSigned.pdf); Pages 10–32: products, certifications, customer mix, capacity and half-year P&L. |\\n| SJUL | [RevisedQ1FY27 business update](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/RAPPID_10072026150526_GBU_Rappid_Signed.pdf); All six pages: correction from revenue to sales; firm backlog 40 crore versus customer confirmations of 11 crore pending purchase orders. |\\n| SSEP | [September21 orderandbacklog filing](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/RAPPID_21092026153828_Work_Order.pdf); All three pages and order annexures; firm backlog 54.36 crore. |\\n| SAUG | [L&T order filing](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/RAPPID_13082026191053_Workorder.pdf); Both pages: ten-week delivery starts after drawing and QAP approval. |\\n| SBHEL | [BHEL FleetSupportShip order](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/RAPPID_15062026102327_Work_Order.pdf); Dated June 13, exchange filing June 15: 18.0576 crore, three shipsets around one year and two in FY27–28. |\\n| SSHREE | [Shree order formalization](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/RAPPID_08062026151443_Work_Order_Shree_.pdf); Formal 8.55 crore order, approximately one-year delivery; supersedes LOI status in June 1 call. |\\n| SPLEDGE | [FY26 annual encumbrance declaration](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/team_sandeshc_29042026124914_40.pdf); April 6 declaration says no encumbrance created during FY26. It is not a September 30 zero-balance statement. |\\n| SCL26 | [January2026 financial filing clarification](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/RAPPID_19012026164650_Clarification_Letter_Signed.pdf); Single business segment; reporting omission acknowledged. Subject says quarter but the clarification concerns the September half-year. |\\n| SCL25 | [June2025 financial filing clarification](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/RAPPID_25062025164758_Claritifcationletterrappidsd.pdf); June 25 signed response admits omitted unmodified-opinion declaration; disputes or seeks guidance on half-year/balancing-figure format queries. |\\n| SPROMISE | [February2025 business guidance](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/RAPPID_01022025120242_RappidQIA.pdf); February 2025 guidance: 50% volume CAGR target for FY26/FY27, foundry talks, and June 2025 capex completion expectation. |\\n| SOBJECT | [IPO objects variation vote](https://www.rappidvalves.in/assets/img/uploads/announcement/16d56-change-in-ipo-object_scrutinizer-result.pdf); All pages: March 31–April 29 postal ballot, April 30 result; inconsistent with annual-report April 17 EGM description. |\\n| SUS | [US qualification status](https://www.rappidvalves.in/assets/img/uploads/announcement/cc2e2-strategic-business-update_rappid_sd.pdf); All four pages: final sampling/testing for product/factory approval; commercial orders not yet assured. |\\n| SSH | [NSE shareholding-pattern table](https://www.nseindia.com/companies-listing/corporate-filings-shareholding-pattern); NSE SME table filtered for RAPPID: September 30 promoter 51.73%, public 48.27%, filed October 12. Earlier failed XML retrieval is superseded by SSHP26. |\\n| SSHXML | [NSE linked September2026 XBRL](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/xbrl/SHP_1736091_12102026034610_WEB.xml); Same primary September XBRL as SSHP26; aggregate contents subsequently verified. No personal shareholder rows retained. |\\n| SMKT | [NSE October19 security master](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/content/cm/interop/NSE_CM_security_19102026.csv.gz); Issuer-only ISIN row. All 60 daily source URLs, complete archive hashes and issuer fields are in market_extract.json. |\\n| SKBL | [KirloskarBrothersFY26 integrated report](https://www.kirloskarpumps.com/virtual-annual-report-2026/); Manufacturing, R&D, distribution and valve-product breadth; qualitative economic benchmark only. |\\n| SHAWA | [HawaEngineersFY25annualreport](https://hawaengltd.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/09/FINAL_Hawa_Engineers_Ltd___AR___2024-25.pdf); Page 28: FY25 revenue 121.0618 crore, EBITDA including other income 6.2397, finance 2.9774, PAT 1.5958. Historical FY25, not current FY26. |\\n| SSHP26 | [NSE September 2026 shareholding XBRL, aggregate facts only](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/xbrl/SHP_1736091_12102026034610_WEB.xml); Quarter ended 30 September 2026; aggregate promoter, encumbrance and lock-in fields |\\n| SATAM | [Atam Valves FY2026 audited standalone results](https://www.atamvalves.in/wp-content/uploads/Atam_Valves_Limited_FinancialResults_31032026.pdf); Physical pages 3–5 P&L, balance sheet and cash flow; limited economic comparison, not full peer audit |\\n\\nThe source register retains document URLs, page/section locators, vintages, and hashes where bytes were obtained. Historical inputs preserve selected, original and restated values rather than replacing conflicts. Full audit observations and related-party amounts are in audit_evidence.json; no source's instructions are treated as authority. The original v3 framework is identified by title and SHA256 in framework_provenance.json, not redistributed.\\n\\nPrincipal unresolved inputs: current cash/deposit access; restrictions and true available overdraft; actual payment/collection calendar; quantitative useful-life benefit; clean comparable historical IC; project commissioning/returns; Vinpra's current relationship; backlog cancellations/margins; current live market restrictions and depth. No current fact is asserted from a post-cutoff document merely because it was easy to retrieve.\\n\\nReconstruction: Python 3.11+ standard library, build_research.py, then verify_research.py. The compact package contains **all** required inputs and code; the full package additionally contains every generated cash path, valuation and attestation. Generated outputs and attestations can be deleted and regenerated without network, raw source files, another company's package or private helpers. Exact input/output hashes and independent checks accompany the deliverables. This is research, not assurance, legal advice, or a promise of returns.\\n\"","\"heading_start\"","\"body_start\"","\"end\"","\"original_heading\"","1606","\"## 1. Executive verdict\\n\"","1655","3893","\"## 2. Research header, gates and data confidence\\n\"","3931","11017","\"## 3. Scorecard and calculation audit\\n\"","11041","12657","\"## 4. Business overview\\n\"","12690","15038","\"## 5. Moat and industry position\\n\"","15069","19234","\"## 6. Revenue growth and scale\\n\"","19279","24394","\"## 7. Growth quality and reinvestment runway\\n\"","24437","27704","\"## 8. Management, governance and execution\\n\"","27730","33571","\"## 9. Financial forensics\\n\"","33612","44230","\"## 10. Valuation and market expectations\\n\"","44275","45651","\"## 11. Bear case and permanent-loss channels\\n\"","45677","46753","\"## 12. 5× / 10× potential\\n\"","46805","48971","\"## 13. Monitoring checklist and falsification tests\\n\"","49022","50726","\"## 14. Final classification and conditional action\\n\"","50773","59973","\"## 15. Sources, assumptions and open questions\\n\"","\"filename\"","\"format\"","\"content_sha256\"","\"scop